  "storylineThread": "Lesson 1: Anchoring phenomenon. Learners shout at a wall or cliff and record what they hear, predict why echoes occur, create the initial Driving Question Board and draw an initial model of the echo.\nLesson 2: Rectilinear propagation. Using the PhET Geometric Optics simulation, pinhole cameras and shadows, learners show that light travels in straight lines and compare how waves travel.\nLesson 3: Reflection of waves, part 1. Learners investigate the laws of reflection with plane mirrors and ripple or simulation demonstrations, and add a reflection component to the model.\nLesson 4: Reflection of sound and echoes. Learners measure the echo delay across a school field, calculate distance using d = vt/2 and compare hard and soft surfaces to explain strong and weak echoes.\nLesson 5: Refraction of waves. Learners watch YouTube demonstrations and investigate the bent straw and apparent depth, relating speed change in a new medium to the change in direction.\nLesson 6: Refraction, Snell's law and lenses. Learners use refractive index calculations and ray diagrams for lenses, linking to spectacles, cameras and microscopes, and revise the model.\nLesson 7: Diffraction of waves. Using the PhET Wave Interference simulation, learners explore waves passing through gaps and around edges, explaining why sound turns corners more readily than light.\nLesson 8: Interference of waves. Learners observe constructive and destructive interference in ripples and simulation, and explain noise-cancelling headphones.\nLesson 9: Stationary waves and resonance. Using the PhET Standing Waves on a String simulation and a string or air-column experiment, learners identify nodes and antinodes and link them to pitch in the nyatiti and flute.\nLesson 10: Doppler effect. Learners watch a YouTube Doppler demonstration, model moving sources and explain the changing pitch of an ambulance siren and its use in radar and astronomy.\nLesson 11: Waves in communication and medicine. Learners use Khan Academy resources on FM modulation to explain radio and mobile signals, and investigate ultrasound imaging.\nLesson 12: Noise pollution and the final model. Learners analyse sources and health effects of noise, design noise-reduction solutions, return to the Driving Question Board and present a final model explaining echoes and other wave behaviours."
