  {
    "number": 7,
    "title": "Diffraction of Waves: Why Sound Turns Corners but Light Does Not",
    "duration": "40 minutes (one double lesson may be used if the simulation needs more time)",
    "substrand": "Sub-Strand 2.1: Properties of Waves",
    "aresKeywords": "diffraction, gap, aperture, edge, wavelength, v = f x wavelength, ripple tank, PhET Wave Interference, doorway, spreading, echo, Hell's Gate, Kisumu hall",
    "slo": {
      "purpose": "Learners investigate how waves spread through gaps and around edges, so that they can explain why the sound of a shout is heard around a building or through a doorway while light seems to travel straight. This adds a new wave behaviour to the class echo model: the sound that leaves the shouting learner does not travel only in one straight beam. It spreads, and that spreading helps explain why the sound is weaker in open places such as the Nairobi grounds and why it fills the Kisumu hall.",
      "knowledge": "Diffraction is the spreading of waves around edges and through gaps. Diffraction is greatest when the gap width is comparable to the wavelength and is small when the gap is much wider than the wavelength. Wavelength is found from v = f x wavelength (lambda = v/f). A 340 Hz sound has lambda = 340/340 = 1.0 m. A 1000 Hz sound has lambda = 340/1000 = 0.34 m. Green light has lambda of about 5.5 x 10^-7 m. A classroom doorway is 1.0 m wide, comparable to the 340 Hz sound wavelength (strong spreading) and enormously larger than the light wavelength (almost no visible bending). Diffraction does not change the frequency or wavelength of the wave.",
      "skills": "Planning and carrying out investigations (NGSS SEP 3): vary gap width and wavelength in the PhET Wave Interference simulation. Analysing and interpreting data (SEP 4): record gap-to-wavelength ratios and the amount of spreading. Using mathematics (SEP 5): calculate lambda = v/f for sound and compare it with the 1.0 m doorway. Developing and using models (SEP 2): add diffraction to the class echo model. Constructing explanations (SEP 6): explain why sound turns corners and light does not.",
      "attitudes": "Curiosity about everyday hearing, such as hearing a voice from the next classroom. Collaboration and respect for peers' ideas during the predict-observe-explain routine. Honesty when reporting simulation results that differ from predictions. Responsibility when sharing roles at the computer or tablet. Appreciation of how physics explains daily life in Kenya.",
      "keyInquiry": "1. How do waves behave when they meet obstacles or change medium? 2. How are the properties of waves applied in day-to-day life? Lesson focus: When a wave meets an edge or a gap, how much does it spread, and what controls the amount?",
      "purposeInStoryline": "Lesson 2 gave learners the ray model, in which light travels in straight lines. Lesson 4 gave them echo measurements at sites A to D and the idea that hard surfaces reflect strongly. Lesson 7 tests the limit of the ray model: Otieno and Wanjiru can hear Mr. Mwangi calling from around a corner even though they cannot see him. Diffraction explains this with wavelength. It prepares Lesson 8 (interference), in which diffracted and reflected waves overlap and meet, and it adds one more reason why a shout on the open Nairobi grounds spreads out and weakens. This lesson does not re-teach reflection or the echo formula; it uses them only as background evidence.",
      "safetyNotes": "Learners work with tablets, computers or a teacher projector, so keep cables tidy and clear of walkways. If a ripple tank is used, wipe up spilt water at once to prevent slips and keep water away from mains sockets. Do not use loud sounds for the doorway demonstration; speak or clap at normal level (about 60 dB, ordinary conversation). Learners with hearing sensitivity may stand slightly farther from the door. Never look directly at the sun or a bright torch beam when comparing light and sound."
    },
    "overview": "Back on campus after the Hell's Gate and Ngong trip, Wanjiru notices that she can hear Mr. Mwangi's voice from behind the classroom block even though she cannot see him. Otieno says: 'In Kisumu, I can hear the assembly from around the corner of the hall too.' Yet when a torch is shone through the same doorway, the light makes a sharp patch with a clear edge. Learners first predict what happens to waves meeting a gap and whether sound and light will behave differently. They then use the PhET Wave Interference simulation (with a ripple tank if available) to change the gap width and wavelength and observe how much the waves spread. They calculate the wavelengths of 340 Hz and 1000 Hz sounds (1.0 m and 0.34 m) and compare them with the 1.0 m doorway and with the wavelength of light (5.5 x 10^-7 m). In the Explain phase they use a claim-evidence-reasoning frame to explain why sound turns corners more than light. Learners add new questions to the Driving Question Board and revise the class model to show that sound spreads as well as reflecting. They connect diffraction back to the phenomenon: the shout does not leave as one narrow beam, and spreading over a large open area helps explain why the Nairobi grounds give no echo.",
    "framework": [
      {
        "phase": "Predict Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Learners gather round the classroom door, which is 1.0 m wide. Mr. Mwangi stands out of sight around the corner outside and speaks, then a learner shines a torch through the doorway onto the floor. Learners hear him clearly but cannot see him, and the torch makes a sharp-edged patch of light. In pairs, then individually in their notebooks, they draw a prediction diagram: a wave (sound and light) approaching a 1.0 m gap, showing what the wave looks like on the other side. They answer: 'Will a wave pass straight through a gap, or spread out? Will sound and light behave the same way? Why?' They link it to the trip: Wanjiru's shout at Hell's Gate left her as a wave, so what happens to the wave as it moves outward in open ground?",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Close your eyes. Where is Mr. Mwangi? Point to him. Now open your eyes. Can you see him?' Then ask: 'Why can we hear someone around a corner but not see them?' WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds in silence before taking any answer. Ask learners to draw before they talk. Cold-call 4 pairs to share one prediction each and write all predictions on the board without judging. Say: 'I am not looking for the right answer yet. I want your best thinking.' Ask: 'What would you need to measure to know which prediction is better?' Use probing: 'Wanjiru said the voice spreads out. What do you think she means?' Leave the unresolved predictions visible for the whole lesson.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Predict-Observe-Explain (POE) with individual drawn predictions: committing to a diagram before instruction exposes learners' initial ideas, such as 'sound goes in straight lines like light' or 'sound is just louder, so it gets round'. Their predictions are revisited in the Explain phase so that they can see their own thinking change.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Check prediction diagrams for: (1) waves drawn continuing as a straight beam (ray-model misconception), (2) waves drawn spreading after the gap, (3) any mention of wavelength or size of gap. Record on a tally on the board how many learners predicted sound and light would behave differently. Look for learners who refer to the earlier ray model of light travelling in straight lines (Lesson 2) or to echoes as sound bouncing back (Lesson 4). Note learners who give no reason as needing support during the Observe phase."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Observe Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "In groups of 3-4 (roles: operator, recorder, timekeeper/checker, reporter), learners open the PhET Wave Interference simulation (water or sound view) or use a ripple tank if one is available. Task A: send plane waves toward a barrier with a gap. Keep the frequency fixed and change the gap width from wide to narrow, recording the amount of spreading on the far side (none, slight, strong). Task B: keep the gap the same and change the wavelength (by changing frequency) to see how spreading changes. Task C: observe waves passing an edge (a single barrier end) and watch the wave bend into the 'shadow' region. Learners then calculate wavelengths using v = f x wavelength: for sound, 340 Hz gives 340/340 = 1.0 m and 1000 Hz gives 340/1000 = 0.34 m. They compare the gap with wavelength in a data table: gap 1.0 m with 1.0 m wavelength (ratio 1, strong spreading); gap 1.0 m with 0.34 m wavelength (gap about 3 wavelengths, less spreading); gap 1.0 m with light of 5.5 x 10^-7 m (gap about 1.8 million wavelengths, practically no spreading). Learners then sketch what they observed at each setting.",
        "teacherMoves": "Circulate and ask: 'What did you change? What stayed the same? What do you see on the far side of the gap?' Say: 'Change one thing at a time, and write down what you see before you move on.' At each group ask the reporter: 'Tell me the gap and the wavelength. Which is bigger?' Prompt: 'Look at where the waves go behind the barrier. Is it empty or are waves there?' Challenge groups that finish early: 'Put the gap at 3 wavelengths. Does the pattern look more like a beam or more like a spreading wave?' WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds after asking: 'What happens to spreading when the gap is much bigger than the wavelength?' Cold-call 3 groups to report one observation each, and write them on the board in a class data table. Check for the common error of confusing wavelength with amplitude. If the simulation is not working, project it from the teacher's computer and let learners direct the settings.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Structured data table with controlled variables: changing one variable at a time (gap width, then wavelength) lets learners find a relationship between spreading and the ratio of gap to wavelength rather than relying on the teacher. The ratio is the key idea that unlocks the sound-versus-light puzzle.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Observe whether groups vary only one factor at a time. Check the data table for: correct lambda calculations (1.0 m and 0.34 m), a statement that narrower gaps or longer wavelengths give more spreading, and the gap-to-wavelength comparison for light. Listen for 'comparable to the wavelength' language. A learner can be marked secure when they can point to the simulation and say, 'When the gap is about the same as the wavelength, the waves spread out most.' Note any group that still draws spreading as a result of a louder wave rather than a wavelength effect."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Explain Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Learners return to their predictions and compare them with the evidence. In pairs they write a claim-evidence-reasoning (CER) explanation answering: 'Why can Wanjiru hear Mr. Mwangi around the corner of the classroom but not see him?' Claim: sound diffracts much more than light. Evidence: the simulation showed strong spreading when the gap was comparable to the wavelength and almost none when the gap was much larger; the 340 Hz sound has lambda = 1.0 m, similar to the 1.0 m doorway, whereas light has lambda = 5.5 x 10^-7 m, far smaller than the gap. Reasoning: diffraction depends on the ratio of wavelength to gap size. They then apply the idea to a new situation: why a low-pitched sound (340 Hz) is heard around a corner better than a high-pitched sound (1000 Hz), and why a matatu's bass is heard from far away behind buildings. They connect to the phenomenon: at the Nairobi open grounds the shout spreads out as it leaves, and in the Kisumu hall sound spreads through the doorway into corridors. They revise their original prediction diagram in a different colour.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Take out your prediction diagram. Use a different colour pen to fix anything the simulation showed you was wrong.' Provide the CER frame on the board: 'I claim ___. My evidence is ___. This is because ___.' WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds for individual writing before pairs compare. Cold-call 3 pairs to read their reasoning and ask 2 other pairs: 'Do you agree? What would you add?' Push: 'You said wavelength matters. Which wavelength: the one for sound or for light? Show me the numbers.' Ask: 'If the doorway were 10 cm wide, what would a 1.0 m sound wave do?' Ask: 'Does diffraction change the speed or the pitch of the sound?' (No: frequency and wavelength stay the same.) Address the misconception that sound is not a ray: 'Sound can also reflect, as you measured at the stone wall, but now we see it also spreads.' Do not re-teach reflection; link only to what learners already know about echoes.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Claim-Evidence-Reasoning (CER) with numerical evidence: requiring learners to cite specific wavelengths (1.0 m, 0.34 m, 5.5 x 10^-7 m) and the 1.0 m doorway moves them from vague statements ('sound is stronger') to a quantitative reason. Applying the idea to a transfer case (low versus high pitch) checks whether the reasoning generalises.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Use a 3-point check of written CER: (1) claim states sound diffracts more than light, (2) evidence includes at least two wavelengths with the doorway width, (3) reasoning links spreading to gap compared with wavelength. Observable indicators of mastery: learners say '1.0 m wave through a 1.0 m gap spreads strongly', and '5.5 x 10^-7 m is tiny compared to the gap so light barely bends'. Exit-ticket question: 'Which spreads more through a 1.0 m doorway: 340 Hz or 1000 Hz sound? Show lambda for each.' Correct answer: 340 Hz (lambda 1.0 m, compared with 0.34 m). Note learners who say high frequency spreads more and re-teach with the simulation next lesson."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Driving Question Board (DQB) Creation",
        "learnerExperience": "Learners revisit the DQB (How do sound waves reflect, and why do some places produce stronger echoes than others?). They write new questions on sticky notes that came from today's work, such as: 'Does diffraction help sound fill the Kisumu hall?', 'If sound spreads out, does it become weaker on the open Nairobi grounds?', 'What happens when diffracted waves from two gaps meet?', 'Why do we hear bass from a matatu before the high notes?' They sort their notes under headings such as 'Already answered', 'Partly answered' and 'Next lessons'. They place one sticky note near the existing questions about the Nairobi grounds and the Kisumu hall to show a new link. They update the class list of evidence: 'Lesson 7: waves spread through gaps and around edges; spreading is greatest when gap is comparable to wavelength; sound (1.0 m) spreads more than light (5.5 x 10^-7 m).'",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Look at the board. Which of our questions can we answer better today? Which new question do you have?' Give each learner one sticky note and WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds in silence to write. Cold-call 4 learners to place and read a question and say which earlier lesson (reflection of sound and echo distance, or the ray model of light) it links to. Ask: 'Which question do you think the next lesson on interference will help us answer?' Group similar questions together with the class. Avoid answering new questions straight away: 'Keep it on the board. We will gather evidence.' Point out questions that connect today's gap-and-wavelength idea to the echo sites, especially sites C and D, and mark them as questions for Lesson 8 and Lesson 12.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Driving Question Board sorting: learners categorise questions as answered, partly answered or open, which makes the growth of knowledge visible and gives them ownership of the next steps. Linking questions to evidence from a specific lesson trains them to treat science as a cumulative process.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Check the sticky notes for questions that use the new vocabulary (diffraction, gap, wavelength) and connect to the phenomenon (echo sites C and D). Observable indicators: learners place at least one new question and can name the evidence that partly answers it. Count how many questions are factual (what is diffraction?) versus explanatory (why does the hall sound muddy if sound also spreads?). Note learners who need prompts to move from recall questions to 'why' and 'how' questions."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Model Building Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Learners revise the class echo model. Amina leads by drawing on large paper the sound of a shout moving away from the learner, the waves spreading as they travel, the sound reflecting at the Hell's Gate cliff and returning, and the sound spreading across the open Nairobi grounds. In groups, learners add a new part to their own model: a labelled diagram of a sound wave (wavelength 1.0 m) passing through a 1.0 m doorway and spreading widely, next to a light wave (5.5 x 10^-7 m) passing through the same doorway as a narrow beam. They write a short caption: 'Diffraction: waves spread around edges and through gaps; most when the gap is comparable to the wavelength.' They also add a note in the margin about how spreading may relate to sites C and D, to be tested later: 'Does spreading help the sound fill the Kisumu hall and weaken in the open Nairobi grounds?' Each learner completes the summary table (observed, learned, explained) in their notebook.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Look at the class model from Lesson 4. What is missing that today's evidence tells us?' WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds, then cold-call 3 learners to suggest one addition. Ask Amina's group to add the doorway diagram to the class model while the other groups complete their own. Prompt: 'Label the wavelength on your drawing. Which is bigger, the gap or the wave?' Ask: 'Which of our four sites is most affected by sound spreading out, and why do you think so?' (Likely D, the open grounds; accept tentative reasons and mark as a hypothesis, not a conclusion.) Remind learners not to include interference yet. Collect one model from each group and photograph or display them. Say: 'Next lesson, we will see what happens when spreading waves meet and overlap.'",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Cumulative model revision with labelled diagrams: adding the new idea (diffraction) to the existing echo model, and marking hypotheses separately from conclusions, helps learners see how each new piece of evidence changes the model. Drawing both sound and light side by side makes the role of wavelength visible.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Check models for: correct labelling of wavelength and gap, sound drawn spreading widely through the 1.0 m gap, light drawn as a narrow beam, a statement that diffraction is greatest when the gap is comparable to the wavelength, and a hypothesis (not a claim) about sites C and D. Observable indicators: learners use the phrase 'comparable to the wavelength' in their captions and show lambda = 1.0 m and 0.34 m values correctly. Review notebook summary tables for accurate entries and give feedback to those who confuse diffraction with reflection."
      }
    ],
    "teacherReflection": "After the lesson, consider: (1) Did learners tie spreading to the ratio of gap to wavelength, or did they remain at 'sound is a wave and light is a ray'? (2) Which prediction diagrams showed that learners think sound goes in a straight beam? (3) Did the wavelength calculations (1.0 m and 0.34 m) cause difficulty with v = f x wavelength? (4) How well did learners keep diffraction separate from reflection when explaining echoes? (5) Which learners were quiet during the CER task, and what support do they need? (6) Did the simulation work on all devices, and was the ripple tank or projector needed? (7) Which sticky-note questions show readiness for interference in Lesson 8? Record any misconceptions, such as 'high-pitched sound spreads more', and plan a short starter activity next lesson to revisit them using the simulation.",
    "summaryTablePrompt": {
      "observed": "We observed that waves passing through a gap spread out on the far side, and that the spreading was greatest when the gap was about the same size as the wavelength. In the simulation, narrow gaps and long wavelengths gave strong spreading, while wide gaps gave a nearly straight beam. At the classroom door, we could hear Mr. Mwangi around the corner but could not see him, and the torch light made a sharp patch.",
      "learned": "We learned that diffraction is the spreading of waves around edges and through gaps. We learned to calculate wavelength using v = f x wavelength: 340 Hz gives 1.0 m and 1000 Hz gives 0.34 m, while green light is about 5.5 x 10^-7 m. A 1.0 m doorway is comparable to the 340 Hz sound wavelength but enormously larger than the wavelength of light. Diffraction does not change frequency or wavelength.",
      "explained": "Sound turns corners more than light because its wavelength (about 1.0 m for 340 Hz) is comparable to the size of doorways and obstacles, so it spreads strongly, while the wavelength of light (5.5 x 10^-7 m) is tiny compared to the gap, so light barely bends and seems to travel in straight lines. This adds to our echo model: the shout spreads as it travels as well as reflecting, which we will test later against the Kisumu hall and the open Nairobi grounds."
    }
  },
  {
    "number": 8,
    "title": "Interference of Waves: When Sound Waves Meet in the Kisumu Hall",
    "duration": "40 minutes (double lesson of 80 minutes if the ripple tank practical is done in full)",
    "substrand": "Sub-Strand 2.1: Properties of Waves",
    "aresKeywords": "interference, superposition, constructive interference, destructive interference, path difference, wavelength, noise-cancelling headphones, reverberation, ripple tank, two-speaker experiment",
    "slo": {
      "purpose": "Learners explain how waves that meet add together (superposition) to give constructive or destructive interference, and use this to explain the muddy overlapping reflections in the Kisumu hall (site C) and the working of noise-cancelling headphones.",
      "knowledge": "Superposition: when two waves meet, the displacements add. Constructive interference happens when the path difference is a whole number of wavelengths (0, 1, 2 ...), giving a larger amplitude and a loud sound. Destructive interference happens when the path difference is an odd number of half wavelengths (0.5, 1.5 ...), giving a smaller amplitude and a quiet sound. For two speakers playing 340 Hz, wavelength = 340/340 = 1.0 m, so a path difference of 1.0 m gives loud and 0.5 m gives quiet. In the Kisumu hall (12 m to the far wall, delay 0.07 s) the overlapping reflections add and cancel at different places, and the sound is loud, muddy and drawn out. Interference is applied in noise-cancelling headphones.",
      "skills": "Observing and describing ripple patterns; predicting and testing with the PhET Sound simulation and LabXchange Wave Interference; using v = f x wavelength to find lambda; calculating path difference and deciding loud or quiet; drawing superposition diagrams; constructing explanations from evidence (NGSS SEP: Planning and Carrying Out Investigations, Developing and Using Models, Constructing Explanations).",
      "attitudes": "Curiosity about how two sounds can make silence; teamwork when sharing observations; respect for others' ideas during discussion; appreciation of how physics protects hearing in Kenyan workplaces, matatus and noisy environments.",
      "keyInquiry": "1. How do waves behave when they meet obstacles or change medium? 2. How are the properties of waves applied in day-to-day life?",
      "purposeInStoryline": "Lesson 4 showed that sound reflects from hard surfaces and that the Kisumu hall wall is only 12 m away (0.07 s delay, less than 0.1 s), and Lesson 7 showed that waves spread around edges and through gaps. Both mean that several waves arrive at the same place at the same time. This lesson answers: what happens when they meet? It gives the next piece of the explanation for why site C sounds muddy and sets up Lesson 9, where a wave and its reflection interfere to form stationary waves.",
      "safetyNotes": "Keep speaker volume low (well below the 85 dB class limit) and never place speakers close to the ears. Wipe up any spilt water around the ripple tank at once and keep mains electrical equipment away from water. Use a power source with a low voltage for ripple tank motors and lamps. Move carefully around the speaker and tape measure lines to avoid tripping. Check that no learner has hearing sensitivity before the tone test, and stop the tone if anyone is uncomfortable."
    },
    "overview": "Mr. Mwangi returns to the DQB question: why is the Kisumu hall muddy and loud while Hell's Gate gives a clean echo? Otieno suggests that many reflections arrive together. Learners predict what happens when two waves meet, then gather evidence from ripples in a ripple tank (or the LabXchange Wave Interference simulation) and from two speakers playing 340 Hz (wavelength 1.0 m) in the PhET Sound simulation and in the classroom. They find loud places where the path difference is 1.0 m and quiet places where it is 0.5 m. In the Explain phase they apply this to the overlapping reflections in the Kisumu hall and explain how noise-cancelling headphones use a wave that is half a wavelength out of step to cancel noise, for example the engine rumble of a bus from Kisumu to Nairobi. Learners add new questions to the DQB and revise the class echo model with a new idea: reflected waves overlap and add, strengthening sound in some places and cancelling it in others.",
    "framework": [
      {
        "phase": "Predict Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Learners recall the four echo sites from the DQB (Hell's Gate 0.50 s, Ngong Hills 1.00 s, Kisumu hall 0.07 s with about 1.2 s of lingering sound, Nairobi grounds no echo). Otieno's observation is read aloud: 'In our hall the voice comes from everywhere and gets muddy.' Learners individually write a prediction in their notebooks: 'When two waves meet at the same point, what will happen to the sound or ripple there? Draw what you think the combined wave looks like.' They then predict whether two speakers playing the same note could ever make a quiet spot. Learners share in pairs and then update their prediction cards with a thumbs-up for 'always louder', thumbs-sideways for 'sometimes louder, sometimes quieter', or thumbs-down for 'no change'.",
        "teacherMoves": "Point to the DQB and say: 'Last lesson we saw waves spread through gaps. Today let us ask: what happens when two waves arrive at the same spot at the same time, like in the Kisumu hall?' Read Otieno's observation. Give 2 minutes of silent writing. Say: 'Draw it. Do not worry about being right; I want your first idea.' Then pose: 'Could two speakers playing the same note ever make a quiet place?' WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds, counting silently. Cold-call 3 learners from different parts of the room (not volunteers) to share predictions and ask 'What made you think that?' Tally thumbs on the board and record the split in a column titled 'Our first ideas'. Do NOT correct predictions; say 'We will test these with evidence.'",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Prediction with Evidence Commitment (Predict-Observe-Explain): learners commit to a drawn prediction before seeing data, so that when the evidence conflicts with 'waves always add to make louder', the contrast creates cognitive dissonance and motivates the superposition model. Drawing the combined wave makes the hidden idea visible.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Check notebooks for: (1) a drawn prediction of the combined wave, not just words; (2) whether learners hold the misconception that two waves always make a louder or bigger wave, or that waves 'collide and stop'; (3) number of thumbs for each option, recorded on the board. Indicator of readiness: at least a third of learners suggest 'sometimes louder, sometimes quieter' or can say why. Note names of learners who think that waves bounce off each other when they meet, to revisit during Observe."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Observe Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Station work in groups of four (about 25 minutes). Station 1: ripple tank (or LabXchange: Wave Interference simulation if no tank is available). Two dippers touch the water at the same rate. Learners sketch the pattern and mark where the water surface moves a lot (constructive) and where it stays nearly flat (destructive), noting that lines of calm water radiate outwards. Station 2: PhET Sound simulation with two speakers set to 340 Hz. Learners move the listener to different positions and record the sound level. They measure distances from each speaker with a tape measure on the screen or classroom floor and calculate the path difference. Using v = f x lambda, they confirm lambda = 340/340 = 1.0 m. They fill a table: path difference 1.0 m gives loud, path difference 0.5 m gives quiet. They also try 0 m (equal distances) and 1.5 m and 2.0 m to see the pattern. Station 3: Kiprono's challenge: two small phone speakers (or a single speaker moved and the sound reflected off a wall) play the same tone; learners walk slowly across the room with eyes closed and notice loud and quiet spots. Station 4 (optional): draw two pulses on tracing paper and overlay them in step and half a wavelength out of step. Learners record 'what I saw' and 'what I measured' columns.",
        "teacherMoves": "Before groups begin, say: 'Your job is to find loud places and quiet places and measure how far each place is from each speaker. Evidence first, explanation later.' Circulate and ask: 'Where on the pattern is the water almost flat? How far is that point from each dipper?' and 'Why do you think the sound dropped here even though both speakers are still on?' Give a warning: 'Keep the volume low and the speaker away from your ears.' When groups find 1.0 m loud and 0.5 m quiet, ask: 'What is the wavelength? How does the path difference compare with it?' WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds after each question before prompting. Check at least one pair at every station. After 20 minutes, cold-call 4 groups (one per station) for a 30-second report of their key data: 'Tell me one number and one observation.' Write the data on the board in a single table: path difference, in wavelengths, result.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Data Table Pattern Finding (Gallery of Evidence): learners collect numerical evidence on path difference and loudness and look for a pattern across stations. Converting 1.0 m and 0.5 m into 'whole' and 'half' wavelengths reveals the rule themselves rather than being told. Using several stations (water, sound, drawing) shows that interference is a general wave behaviour, not only a sound effect.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Observable indicators: (1) learners calculate lambda = 340/340 = 1.0 m correctly using v = f x lambda; (2) the table has path difference 1.0 m marked loud and 0.5 m marked quiet; (3) ripple sketches show alternating bright and calm lines; (4) groups can say that the speakers were still both on in the quiet spot. Teacher checklist: tick each group that links path difference to loudness. Ask struggling groups to compare path difference with the wavelength by dividing (0.5/1.0 = half a wavelength)."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Explain Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Whole-class sensemaking. Learners use their data to write the rule in their own words: 'When two waves meet, their displacements add (superposition). If crest meets crest (path difference 0, 1.0 m, 2.0 m ... whole wavelengths) the wave is bigger: constructive interference, loud. If crest meets trough (path difference 0.5 m, 1.5 m ... half wavelengths) they cancel: destructive interference, quiet.' Learners draw two waves on the board in step and out of step and add them point by point. Then apply the idea to two situations. (1) Kisumu hall (site C): the speaker is 12 m from the far wall and the delay is 0.07 s, so the echo arrives less than 0.1 s after the original and overlaps it. Walls, floor and roof give many reflected waves that meet. At some places they add (loud), at others they partly cancel, and the changing mix with the 1.2 s lingering sound makes the voice loud, muddy and drawn out. Learners link this to Lesson 4 where the delay was less than 0.1 s. (2) Noise-cancelling headphones: a tiny microphone picks up the noise, for example a matatu with 95 dB music or a bus engine. The electronics produce a wave of the same frequency but half a wavelength out of step (crest where the noise has a trough), so when the two meet at the ear they cancel, and the noise is much quieter. Learners complete a short worked example: speakers at 340 Hz, listener 5.0 m from one speaker and 5.5 m from the other. Path difference 0.5 m is half a wavelength, so quiet. They also decide what would happen at path difference 2.0 m (loud).",
        "teacherMoves": "Ask: 'Looking at your table, what is the link between path difference and what we hear?' WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds, then cold-call 3 learners to complete the sentence 'Loud happens when the path difference is ...' and 'Quiet happens when ...'. Draw two sine waves on the board and add them with a different colour chalk, narrating: 'Crest plus crest gives a bigger crest; crest plus trough gives zero.' Then ask: 'Otieno says the Kisumu hall is muddy. Using interference and the 0.07 s delay, can you explain why?' Use Think-Pair-Share: 1 minute thinking, 2 minutes pairs, then cold-call 4 pairs. Press for evidence: 'Which number tells you the echo overlaps the voice?' (0.07 s is less than 0.1 s). Then show how noise-cancelling headphones work and ask: 'If the noise is 340 Hz, how far out of step must the headphone's wave be?' (half a wavelength, 0.5 m). Correct the misconception 'the sound energy disappears' by saying: 'The waves still meet; the displacements cancel at that point, and energy is redistributed to the loud places.' Assign the worked example (5.0 m and 5.5 m) and walk around to check.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Claim-Evidence-Reasoning with Model Application: learners state a claim (e.g. 'the hall is muddy because reflections overlap and interfere'), cite evidence (0.07 s delay, path difference table, 1.2 s lingering) and give reasoning (superposition). Applying the same rule to a new context, the headphones, tests transfer and shows that the same wave idea explains both a natural phenomenon and a technology.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Indicators: (1) learners state both conditions correctly (whole wavelengths loud, half wavelengths quiet) using numbers from the data; (2) the worked example answer is 'path difference 0.5 m, half a wavelength, destructive, quiet'; (3) learners link the 0.07 s delay to the overlap in site C and do not claim that the Kisumu hall has no reflection; (4) learners explain headphones as 'a second wave half a wavelength out of step' and not as 'blocking' the sound. Use a three-question exit slip: path difference 1.0 m, path difference 1.5 m, and one sentence on headphones. Mark: correct, partly correct, not yet."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Driving Question Board (DQB) Creation",
        "learnerExperience": "Learners revisit the class DQB and, in groups, write new questions on sticky notes arising from today's evidence, for example: 'Why does the Kisumu hall sound loud in some seats and quiet in others?', 'Can interference make a quiet spot at Hell's Gate if we shout from two places?', 'How does an ambulance siren on Waiyaki Way sound different as it passes?', 'What happens when a wave and its reflection interfere on a nyatiti string?' Learners sort the notes into 'answered today', 'partly answered' and 'for later lessons'. They place the notes beside the original DQB question: How do sound waves reflect, and why do some places produce stronger echoes than others?",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Write one question that today's evidence made you wonder. Start with how or why.' Allow 3 minutes. Circulate and ask 'What did you see or hear that made you ask this?' Cold-call 3 groups to read one question each and ask the class: 'Which lesson do you think will help us answer it?' Gently steer the question about a wave and its reflection to Lesson 9 and the ambulance question to Lesson 10, saying: 'Hold that thought; we will meet it soon.' Move answered questions (e.g. 'Why is the Kisumu hall muddy?') to the 'answered, partly' column and ask a learner to write the evidence beside it. WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds before accepting responses to the sorting question.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Question Sorting on the DQB (Student-Generated Questions): turning today's evidence into new questions keeps learners as owners of the inquiry. Sorting into answered, partly answered and future shows progress and links each lesson to the unit's driving question, making coherence visible.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Indicators: each group writes at least one question that uses interference vocabulary correctly; at least two questions connect to the phenomenon (Kisumu hall, Hell's Gate, Nairobi grounds); learners can justify placing a question in the answered, partly or later column. Record questions that reveal a misconception (e.g. 'Where does the sound go in the quiet spot?') and address these at the start of Lesson 9."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Model Building Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Learners revise the class echo model started in Lesson 1, adding interference. In groups (Amina's drawing role rotates among members) they draw a labelled diagram of the Kisumu hall (site C): a speaker 12 m from the far wall, the direct sound, reflected sound from the wall, roof and floor, and the places where the waves meet. They add labels: 'delay 0.07 s, less than 0.1 s: echo merges with voice', 'many reflections add (constructive) and partly cancel (destructive)', 'sound lingers about 1.2 s: reverberation'. Beside it they draw a small panel for Hell's Gate (85 m, 0.50 s, a single clean reflection, so little overlap). A third panel shows a noise-cancelling headphone: noise wave, anti-noise wave half a wavelength out of step, and the near-silent result. Each group adds an arrow saying 'what is new in our model today' and writes a one-sentence explanation of why Hell's Gate echoes are clear but Kisumu hall sound is muddy.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Open your model from Lesson 4. What is missing that explains what the waves do when they meet?' Hand out large paper. Circulate asking: 'Where in your hall diagram do waves meet? What decides whether they add or cancel?' and 'Why does Hell's Gate give a clean echo and not a muddy one?' WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds before following up. Remind groups to keep the numbers exactly: 12 m, 0.07 s, 0.1 s, about 1.2 s. After 12 minutes, run a gallery walk: each group leaves one member at the model and the others visit two other groups. Cold-call 3 visitors: 'What is one idea you saw in another group's model that we should add to ours?' Then complete the class model on the wall together and have learners copy the new interference box in their notebooks. Close by asking: 'What is still unexplained?' to point towards stationary waves in Lesson 9.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Cumulative Model Revision with Gallery Walk: learners add interference to the existing echo model, so earlier ideas (reflection, delay, surfaces, diffraction) and the new idea of superposition fit together in one explanation. Peer critique in the gallery walk exposes gaps and strengthens the reasoning.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Observable indicators: (1) the hall diagram shows multiple reflected paths meeting, not only one echo; (2) the labels use 0.07 s and 0.1 s correctly; (3) both constructive and destructive interference are named and linked to path difference of whole and half wavelengths; (4) the headphone panel shows a wave half a wavelength out of step; (5) the model distinguishes echo (site A, B) from reverberation (site C). Use a 4-point checklist per group and note groups that need support before Lesson 9."
      }
    ],
    "teacherReflection": "After the lesson, reflect: Did learners move from 'two waves always add to a louder wave' to 'path difference decides loud or quiet'? Which evidence (ripple tank, PhET, walking test) convinced them most? Did anyone suggest the sound 'disappears' at quiet spots, and how did I respond? Were the numbers 340 Hz, 1.0 m and 0.5 m used accurately? Did learners link the 0.07 s delay in the Kisumu hall to interference without re-teaching reflection from Lesson 4 or diffraction from Lesson 7? How equitably did cold-calling include quieter learners? What do I need to adjust, such as giving more time with the tape measure or simpler wave drawings, before Lesson 9 on stationary waves?",
    "summaryTablePrompt": {
      "observed": "In the ripple tank and in the PhET Sound simulation with two speakers at 340 Hz, we observed alternating regions of large movement or loud sound and regions of almost no movement or quiet sound. At path difference 1.0 m (lambda = 1.0 m) the sound was loud and at 0.5 m it was quiet.",
      "learned": "Waves that meet add their displacements (superposition). Whole-wavelength path differences give constructive interference (loud) and half-wavelength path differences give destructive interference (quiet). Noise-cancelling headphones use a wave half a wavelength out of step with the noise.",
      "explained": "In the Kisumu hall (site C) the far wall is 12 m away, the delay is 0.07 s (less than 0.1 s), so reflections overlap the voice and each other. They add in some places and cancel in others, and the many hard surfaces keep the sound lingering for about 1.2 s, so it is loud, muddy and drawn out. At Hell's Gate there is a single clean reflection after 0.50 s, so the echo is distinct."
    }
  },
  {
    "number": 9,
    "title": "Stationary Waves and Resonance: Nodes, Antinodes and the Pitch of the Nyatiti and Flute",
    "duration": "40 minutes (double lesson of 80 minutes recommended if the string or air-column experiment is done by every group)",
    "substrand": "Sub-Strand 2.1: Properties of Waves",
    "aresKeywords": "stationary wave, node, antinode, resonance, fundamental frequency, harmonic, pitch, nyatiti, flute, reflection at ends, superposition, f = v/2L",
    "slo": {
      "purpose": "To help learners explain how a wave and its reflection interfere in a string or air column to form a stationary wave with nodes and antinodes, and to link the set frequencies f = v/2L to resonance and to the pitch of the nyatiti and flute. This extends the echo phenomenon: reflection does not only send sound back to the ear, it can also trap and reinforce waves between two ends.",
      "knowledge": "Learners will: (a) state that a stationary wave forms when a wave and its reflection, travelling in opposite directions, interfere (builds on Lesson 3: reflection, and Lesson 8: superposition giving constructive and destructive interference); (b) identify nodes as points of zero amplitude and antinodes as points of maximum amplitude; (c) use v = f x wavelength and wavelength = 2L for the fundamental to calculate f = v/2L; (d) calculate the nyatiti fundamental (0.60 m string, 120 m/s: wavelength 1.20 m, f = 100 Hz) and second harmonic (200 Hz, 3 nodes including the ends, 2 antinodes); (e) calculate the flute fundamental (open tube 0.34 m, wavelength 0.68 m, f = 340/0.68 = 500 Hz); (f) describe resonance as large-amplitude vibration when a driving frequency matches a natural frequency; (g) state that pitch depends on frequency, so a shorter string or tube gives a higher pitch.",
      "skills": "Science and Engineering Practices: Developing and using models (drawing and revising stationary-wave diagrams); Planning and carrying out investigations (varying string length or tube length and measuring frequency); Analysing and interpreting data (comparing predicted and measured frequencies); Using mathematics (v = f x wavelength, f = v/2L); Constructing explanations (why only certain frequencies make stable patterns). Core competencies: critical thinking and problem solving, communication and collaboration, digital literacy (PhET Standing Waves on a String).",
      "attitudes": "Learners will appreciate the physics behind Kenyan musical heritage such as the Luo nyatiti, show curiosity about links between echoes and musical instruments, work responsibly and respectfully in groups, and value evidence and careful measurement. Values: responsibility, respect, unity. PCIs: Cultural heritage (valuing indigenous instruments), Safety in using equipment.",
      "keyInquiry": "How do waves behave when they meet obstacles or change medium? How are the properties of waves applied in day-to-day life? (Reflection at the fixed ends of a string or tube produces stationary waves, which are applied in musical instruments such as the nyatiti and flute.)",
      "purposeInStoryline": "Lessons 3 and 4 showed that sound reflects from a surface and returns as an echo. Lesson 8 showed that waves that meet add by superposition. Lesson 9 combines these ideas: when a wave is reflected back along the same path, the incident and reflected waves overlap and make a stationary pattern. This explains why the Kisumu hall can ring or boom at some notes (resonance), prepares for the Doppler effect in Lesson 10 by fixing the link between frequency, wavelength and pitch, and adds a resonance piece to the final model in Lesson 12.",
      "safetyNotes": "Keep strings at low tension so they cannot snap and hit eyes; wear safety glasses or keep faces away from the string line. Do not blow hard into tubes or share flute mouthpieces without wiping them with a clean cloth. Keep speaker or signal-generator volume below 70 dB and limit loud pure tones to a few seconds (safe limit in class 85 dB). Keep electrical equipment away from water. Tie back long hair near clamps. Handle the nyatiti gently as a cultural object and ask the owner or resource person before touching."
    },
    "overview": "Mr. Mwangi opens by recalling the trip: the learners' shouts bounced off the Hell's Gate cliff and came back after 0.50 s. He asks what happens when a wave is reflected and trapped between two fixed ends, like the 0.60 m string on a nyatiti. Learners first predict what a vibrating string looks like and what note it will give. They then gather evidence in the PhET Standing Waves on a String simulation and a hands-on string or air-column experiment (a long rope or elastic cord fixed at one end, or a tube of varying length with a tuning fork), identifying nodes and antinodes and recording harmonics. In the Explain phase they use v = f x wavelength and wavelength = 2L to calculate the nyatiti (100 Hz and 200 Hz) and flute (500 Hz) frequencies and use superposition to explain why only certain patterns survive (resonance). They add questions and evidence to the Driving Question Board (DQB) and revise the class model so that reflection both sends sound back as an echo and, when trapped between two ends, builds stationary waves.",
    "framework": [
      {
        "phase": "Predict Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "(8 minutes) Wanjiru recalls shouting at the Hell's Gate cliff and hearing her voice return after 0.50 s. Mr. Mwangi shows a nyatiti (or a picture/video) with a vibrating length of 0.60 m and plucks one string. Learners individually sketch in their notebooks what they think the plucked string looks like at its fastest vibration (a snapshot or a blurred shape) and predict: (1) How many points on the string do not move at all? (2) What will happen to the pitch if the string is made shorter, as when the player presses it? (3) Will a flute, which has no strings, also make notes by a similar process? Learners then share in pairs, using the sentence frame: 'I think the string will look like ... because the wave ...' They also link to the echo: 'When a wave reaches the fixed end of the string, I think it ...'",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Remember the cliff at Hell's Gate. The sound hit the rock and came back. Now picture a wave on this nyatiti string hitting the fixed end. What do you think it does?' Pluck the string once and ask learners to watch closely. Give 2 minutes of silent individual drawing, then say: 'Draw what you think the shape looks like at the moment of widest swing. Mark any place you think stays still.' WAIT TIME: 10-15 seconds after asking 'What happens to the wave at the fixed end?' before taking any answer. Cold-call 4 learners (including Otieno's group and Kiprono's group) to share their sketches without telling them if they are right or wrong. Record 3 distinct predictions on the board labelled P1, P2, P3. Ask: 'What would you need to see to decide which prediction is correct?' Do not correct misconceptions such as 'the wave travels along the string and disappears' or 'all parts move equally'.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Predict-Observe-Explain (POE) with initial model drawing: Learners commit to a prediction before seeing evidence, which exposes their existing ideas (for example, that a wave on a string simply travels away like a shout away from the learner). Linking it to reflection (Lesson 3) and interference (Lesson 8) activates prior knowledge so that later evidence can be compared with their own ideas. Making a sketch (Developing and using models) makes thinking visible for the teacher and for peers.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Observable indicators: (a) at least 80% of learners have a labelled sketch showing a string shape and marking where they think the string stays still; (b) learners use reflection language such as 'the wave bounces back from the end' when asked about the fixed end; (c) record on a checklist which learners predict that shorter length gives higher pitch. Look for misconceptions: 'the wave is lost at the end', 'the whole string moves the same amount', 'pitch depends on how hard it is plucked' (loudness and pitch confused). Note the names of learners with these ideas to revisit during the Observe phase."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Observe Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "(Part of 20 minutes in total for observing) Station A (PhET: Standing Waves on a String, or a phone/projector demonstration if computers are scarce): groups of 3-4 run the simulation, set the string to fixed ends, change the frequency slowly, and watch for the frequencies at which a clear stable pattern forms. They record in a table: number of loops, number of nodes (including ends), number of antinodes, and relative frequency (f, 2f, 3f). They observe that between those frequencies the pattern is messy and small. Station B (hands-on): either (i) a 3-4 m rope or elastic cord fixed at one end and shaken by hand at the other, or a 0.60 m taut string or wire on a sonometer/nyatiti; or (ii) an air column: a tube or measuring cylinder with water, a tuning fork (for example 500 Hz or 512 Hz) and the water level adjusted until the sound suddenly becomes loud (resonance). Learners mark nodes with chalk or tape and antinodes with a different mark, measure length L with a tape measure, and compare fundamental and second harmonic. They also measure the pitch change when they press the string to make it shorter (like a nyatiti player) or use a shorter tube (like covering flute holes). Kiprono times the vibrations with a stopwatch where possible, Amina draws the patterns, Otieno reads measurements, Wanjiru checks the table and asks 'Why only these frequencies?'",
        "teacherMoves": "Set up stations before the lesson and test the simulation. Say: 'Your job is to find the frequencies where the string shape stops moving along and just stays in place, with some points that never move.' Circulate and ask each group: 'Show me a point that does not move. What do we call it? Show me a point with the biggest swing.' Use the language: node = zero amplitude, antinode = maximum amplitude. Prompt: 'Count the nodes including the two ends. Count the antinodes. What do you notice as the frequency goes up?' WAIT TIME: 10-15 seconds after asking 'What do you see happening between the loops?' Cold-call 3 groups to report their node and antinode counts for the first and second patterns. Challenge groups that finish early: 'Predict the pattern for three times the lowest frequency, then test it.' Ask: 'Which of your observations looks like the pattern when a wave and its reflection overlap, like Lesson 8?' Safety: remind about low string tension, safety glasses and low volume. Collect data on a class chart: fundamental (1 loop, 2 nodes, 1 antinode); second harmonic (2 loops, 3 nodes, 2 antinodes); third harmonic (3 loops, 4 nodes, 3 antinodes). Show the nyatiti string case: L = 0.60 m, fundamental pattern one loop, second harmonic two loops with 3 nodes including the ends and 2 antinodes.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Data table and pattern-seeking (Analysing and interpreting data): Learners collect nodes, antinodes and frequency into a shared table so the pattern emerges for themselves (each higher pattern has one more loop and a frequency that is a whole-number multiple of the lowest). Modelling with physical and simulated systems lets them see the wave and its reflection superpose. Noticing and wondering: learners record 'I notice ...' and 'I wonder ...' statements, for example 'I wonder why the Kisumu hall sometimes booms on certain notes', connecting back to the echo phenomenon.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Observable indicators: (a) each group's table correctly shows 2 nodes and 1 antinode for the fundamental and 3 nodes and 2 antinodes for the second harmonic; (b) learners correctly label a node and an antinode on their sketch; (c) learners state that a shorter string or tube gives a higher pitch, based on their measurement; (d) groups find the resonance point in the air column (loud sound) and measure L within about 1 cm of the expected value; (e) quick thumbs check: 'Hold up one finger for node, two fingers for antinode' when the teacher points to positions on a displayed wave. Note groups that confuse nodes with the ends only or think nodes are the places where the string moves most."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Explain Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "(Part of 20 minutes, continuing the lesson) In their groups, learners use their data to build a causal explanation in three steps. Step 1 (Why a pattern forms): the wave travelling along the string reflects at the fixed end (reflection, Lesson 3) and meets the incoming wave; the two waves superpose (Lesson 8): where they arrive in step the displacement doubles (antinodes, constructive interference) and where they arrive opposite they cancel (nodes, destructive interference), so the pattern stays in place instead of travelling. Step 2 (Why only certain frequencies): a stable pattern forms only when a whole number of half-wavelengths fits the length, so for the fundamental wavelength = 2L and f = v/2L. Step 3 (Calculations and pitch): Nyatiti: L = 0.60 m, v = 120 m/s, wavelength = 2 x 0.60 = 1.20 m, f = 120/1.20 = 100 Hz; second harmonic has wavelength 0.60 m, f = 120/0.60 = 200 Hz (3 nodes including the ends, 2 antinodes). Flute modelled as an open tube L = 0.34 m: wavelength = 2L = 0.68 m, f = 340/0.68 = 500 Hz (using v = 340 m/s). Learners compare their experiment with calculations and explain pitch: the shorter the string or tube, the higher the frequency and the higher the pitch; loudness depends on amplitude, not frequency. They define resonance: when a driving frequency matches a natural frequency (100 Hz, 200 Hz for the nyatiti string), the vibration grows large, as when a tuning fork makes the air column loud. They connect to the phenomenon: reflection from the hall walls and roof in Kisumu can make some notes boom, and a closed space acts like a tube or string whose natural frequencies are strengthened. Learners write a CER statement (Claim, Evidence, Reasoning): 'The nyatiti string of length 0.60 m gives 100 Hz because ...'.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Let's build the explanation together. First, what two waves are on the string at the same time?' WAIT TIME: 10-15 seconds before calling on anyone. Cold-call 3 learners to name the incident wave and the reflected wave, then 2 learners to describe what happens at a node and at an antinode using the words 'cancel' and 'add'. Ask: 'Why can the string not vibrate at just any frequency and still hold a steady pattern?' Draw the fundamental and second harmonic on the board with L marked, and ask learners to say aloud how many half-wavelengths fit in L each time. Model the calculation for the nyatiti on the board: 'wavelength = 2L = 1.20 m, then f = v divided by wavelength = 120 divided by 1.20 = 100 Hz.' Then ask groups to calculate the second harmonic and the flute, with the flute done on mini-whiteboards and shown on the count of three. Cold-call 4 groups for their answers (200 Hz and 500 Hz) and ask them to show the units. Ask: 'If Nyatiti player presses the string halfway along, L becomes 0.30 m. Before calculating, will the pitch go up or down? Then calculate: wavelength 0.60 m and f = 200 Hz.' Address the misconception that nodes are places where there is no wave at all: 'The wave is still there, but the two waves cancel at that point.' Stress that this lesson builds on reflection and interference and does not re-teach them. Connect to the phenomenon: 'In Kisumu hall, hard walls reflect sound back and forth. Why might some notes sound extra loud there?' Accept answers about resonance of the hall.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Claim-Evidence-Reasoning (CER) with mathematical modelling: Learners state a claim about the frequency, support it with measurements from the experiment and simulation, and give reasoning using superposition, reflection at the ends and v = f x wavelength. This forces them to link observations, mathematics and mechanism. Talk moves (revoicing, 'add on', 'who agrees or disagrees and why?') build collective explanation. Using the same wave equation for the nyatiti, flute and echo problems shows that one set of ideas explains many situations, supporting transfer.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Observable indicators: (a) learners correctly calculate nyatiti fundamental 100 Hz, second harmonic 200 Hz and flute 500 Hz, with correct units and working shown (wavelength = 2L first, then f = v/wavelength); (b) CER statements mention reflection at the ends AND interference (superposition) as the cause of stationary waves; (c) learners correctly state that pitch depends on frequency and that a shorter length gives higher pitch; (d) learners define resonance with the idea of matching frequencies and larger amplitude; (e) exit question on a slip: 'Name one node-antinode difference and give the fundamental frequency of the nyatiti string.' Aim for at least 75% correct; plan a short re-teach with a diagram for those who use L instead of 2L as the wavelength."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Driving Question Board (DQB) Creation",
        "learnerExperience": "(5 minutes) Learners return to the Driving Question Board: 'How do sound waves reflect, and why do some places produce stronger echoes than others?' Each group writes on sticky notes: (1) one new piece of evidence from today (for example 'a wave and its reflection on a 0.60 m string make a stationary wave with 2 nodes at the ends and 1 antinode in the middle, giving 100 Hz'); (2) one question they can now answer from earlier lessons (for example how a reflected wave can be trapped and reinforced); (3) one new question (for example 'Does the Kisumu hall have natural frequencies that make some notes boom?', 'Why does a flute sound different from a nyatiti at the same pitch?', 'Can reflected sound at Hell's Gate ever form a stationary wave?'). They place notes under categories such as Reflection, Surfaces, Stationary waves and resonance, and Still to find out.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Look at our DQB. Which questions can we now answer or partly answer after today?' WAIT TIME: 10-15 seconds before taking responses. Cold-call 3 learners to read a sticky note aloud and say which lesson evidence supports it. Ask: 'Which of our earlier questions about the muddy Kisumu hall does resonance help with?' Move answered questions to an 'Answered/Partly answered' area and mark partly answered ones with a star. Add a teacher-prepared question if needed: 'Why do some notes boom in a hall with hard walls and a roof?' Highlight links to the next lesson: 'Next time we will ask what happens when the source itself is moving, like the ambulance on Waiyaki Way.' Photograph the DQB for records and for learners who are absent.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Driving Question Board as a living record of understanding: Learners connect new evidence to the driving question, see their growing understanding and own the questions that drive the unit. Sorting questions into 'answered', 'partly answered' and 'new' encourages metacognition, and explicitly linking evidence to a lesson (Lesson 3 reflection, Lesson 8 interference, Lesson 9 stationary waves) builds a coherent storyline rather than a set of separate topics.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Observable indicators: (a) each group posts at least one evidence note and one new question; (b) at least half of the new questions refer to resonance, nodes or stationary waves in connection with the echo sites, for example the Kisumu hall; (c) learners can state which lesson supports each answered question; (d) record questions that show misconceptions (for example 'does the sound stop at a node?') for the next lesson. Listen for use of the words node, antinode, resonance and frequency."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Model Building Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "(7 minutes) Amina leads each group in revising the class echo model drawn in earlier lessons (shout leaves learner, travels as a wave, reflects from a surface following the law of reflection, returns after t = 2d/340). Groups add a new panel showing a wave trapped between two fixed ends (a nyatiti string 0.60 m or a flute tube 0.34 m): incident wave and reflected wave in opposite directions, nodes (N) and antinodes (A) labelled, and wavelength = 2L for the fundamental with f = v/2L. They add a short caption: 'Reflection at the ends plus interference gives a stationary wave. Only certain frequencies fit. Resonance happens when the driving frequency matches one of them.' They also add a 'What the model cannot yet explain' box (for example why the Kisumu hall sounds loud and muddy; connecting resonance and reverberation will be refined in Lesson 12). A volunteer from each of two groups compares models and checks that numbers match the established data: nyatiti 100 Hz and 200 Hz, flute 500 Hz.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Our model so far explains how a shout goes out and comes back as an echo. What new piece does today's evidence add?' WAIT TIME: 10-15 seconds before groups start drawing. Provide a model checklist on the board: (1) source and wave path, (2) reflection at a boundary, (3) two waves overlapping, (4) nodes and antinodes labelled, (5) frequency or pitch link with numbers, (6) a limitation. Circulate and ask: 'Where on your drawing is the reflected wave? Where do the two waves cancel? What would change if the string were shorter?' Cold-call 3 groups to explain their new panel in under 30 seconds each; ask the class to give one 'glow' (what is right) and one 'grow' (what to improve). Ask: 'Does your model still explain the echo at Hell's Gate with a 0.50 s delay? Does the new part explain the Kisumu hall completely?' Encourage honest limits: 'We have a piece of the Kisumu puzzle, not all of it.' Keep the class model on the wall for Lesson 10.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Cumulative model revision (Developing and using models): Learners extend a single class model rather than starting afresh, so each lesson adds a mechanism. Peer critique with 'glow and grow' and a limitations box models how scientists test and refine ideas. Linking the string and tube to the hall shows that models can transfer across contexts.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Observable indicators: (a) models include both the incident and reflected waves and label at least one node and one antinode correctly; (b) the fundamental diagram shows wavelength = 2L with L marked; (c) frequencies for the nyatiti (100 Hz and 200 Hz) and flute (500 Hz) are correct on the model; (d) a limitation is listed that honestly identifies something not yet explained about the Kisumu hall; (e) peers' feedback uses evidence from the investigation. Use a 3-2-1 exit ticket if time allows: 3 key terms, 2 equations, 1 question left."
      }
    ],
    "teacherReflection": "Before the next lesson, consider: (1) Did learners connect stationary waves to reflection (Lesson 3) and interference (Lesson 8), or did they treat today as an isolated topic? Check CER statements for both ideas. (2) Which learners confused nodes with the ends only, or used L instead of 2L as the wavelength? Plan a quick correction with a diagram. (3) Did the PhET simulation and the hands-on station give consistent results, and where did measured frequencies differ from calculated ones (tension, end effects, measurement error)? Use that discussion to teach about reliable data. (4) Was the nyatiti used respectfully and did the lesson value Kenyan heritage and any local musicians who could visit as resource persons? (5) How well did the wait time work, and did cold-calling bring out quiet learners? (6) Are learners ready for the Doppler effect in Lesson 10, where frequency and pitch change because of motion? Ensure they are secure with v = f x wavelength and the idea that pitch depends on frequency. (7) Record any new DQB questions about the Kisumu hall to revisit in Lessons 10 to 12.",
    "summaryTablePrompt": {
      "observed": "We observed that a plucked or shaken string fixed at both ends (and an air column in a tube) forms steady patterns at particular frequencies only. In these patterns some points never move (nodes) and some swing the most (antinodes). The fundamental has 2 nodes (the ends) and 1 antinode; the second harmonic has 3 nodes and 2 antinodes. A shorter string or tube gave a higher pitch, and the air column became loud at a particular length (resonance).",
      "learned": "We learned that a stationary wave forms when a wave and its reflection travel in opposite directions and interfere: they add at antinodes and cancel at nodes. For the fundamental, wavelength = 2L, so f = v/2L. Pitch depends on frequency, and resonance happens when a driving frequency matches a natural frequency of the string or air column.",
      "explained": "We can explain that the nyatiti string 0.60 m long with wave speed 120 m/s has wavelength 1.20 m and fundamental f = 120/1.20 = 100 Hz, with a second harmonic of 200 Hz, and that a flute modelled as a 0.34 m open tube has wavelength 0.68 m and f = 340/0.68 = 500 Hz. Reflection at the ends plus interference gives stationary waves, and in an echo situation such as the Kisumu hall reflected sound can also build up at some frequencies, which adds to our model of why some places sound stronger than others."
    }
  },
  {
    "number": 10,
    "title": "Doppler Effect: Why the Ambulance Siren Changes Pitch as It Passes",
    "duration": "40 minutes",
    "substrand": "Sub-Strand 2.1: Properties of Waves",
    "aresKeywords": "Doppler effect, observed frequency, relative motion, pitch, wavelength, source, observer, radar, redshift, ambulance siren, Waiyaki Way",
    "slo": {
      "purpose": "Learners explain that when a sound source and an observer move relative to each other, the observed frequency changes: higher when they approach and lower when they recede. They link this to v = f x wavelength and apply it to the ambulance siren, radar and astronomy, and ask what it adds to the echo story.",
      "knowledge": "By the end of the lesson the learner should be able to: a) state that relative motion of source and observer changes the observed frequency (higher when approaching, lower when receding); b) use v = f x wavelength to explain that waves bunch up ahead of a moving source and stretch out behind it; c) interpret the ambulance data: 700 Hz emitted, 744 Hz approaching, 661 Hz receding at 20 m/s; d) describe uses of the Doppler effect in radar and astronomy.",
      "skills": "Developing and using models (wavefront drawings and a human-model walk-through); analysing and interpreting data (comparing 700, 744 and 661 Hz); constructing explanations; using mathematics (v = f x wavelength); obtaining and communicating information (YouTube Doppler demonstration).",
      "attitudes": "Curiosity about everyday sounds such as sirens and matatus; respect for classmates' ideas during discussion; responsibility in group roles; appreciation of how Physics is used in road safety, weather and space science. Core competencies: communication and collaboration, critical thinking, digital literacy. Values: responsibility, respect.",
      "keyInquiry": "How do waves behave when they meet obstacles or change medium? How are the properties of waves applied in day-to-day life?",
      "purposeInStoryline": "Lessons 7 to 9 gave learners frequency, wavelength and pitch linked by v = f x wavelength. Lesson 10 adds a new wave behaviour: the frequency heard changes when the source or observer is moving. Echoes at Hell's Gate, Ngong Hills, the Kisumu hall and the Nairobi grounds involve a reflecting wall, but the Doppler effect shows that motion alone changes pitch, and a moving reflector also returns a shifted frequency. This is the idea behind radar and prepares Lesson 11 on communication and medicine. Learners add a Doppler panel to the class model and a new question to the DQB.",
      "safetyNotes": "Do the human-model walk-through in a clear open space away from desks. Learners walk, never run. For any outdoor siren observation, stay well away from the road, especially Waiyaki Way style busy roads. Keep any speaker or phone tone at a low, comfortable volume, well below the 85 dB class limit. Never look at the Sun when discussing astronomy."
    },
    "overview": "Mr. Mwangi recalls the Hell's Gate trip: on the road back to Nairobi an ambulance passed the bus on Waiyaki Way, and its siren seemed to drop in pitch as it went by. Wanjiru asks whether the siren itself changed. Learners predict, watch a YouTube Doppler demonstration, then model the moving source with wavefront drawings and a human wave walk. They use v = f x wavelength with the siren data (700 Hz emitted, 744 Hz approaching, 661 Hz receding at 20 m/s) to explain the change, and connect the idea to radar speed guns and the redshift of stars. They then update the Driving Question Board and class model, noting that sound can return changed in pitch as well as delayed.",
    "framework": [
      {
        "phase": "Predict Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Learners recall the bus ride back from Hell's Gate when an ambulance passed on Waiyaki Way. Mr. Mwangi plays or imitates a siren rising then falling in pitch. Individually, learners write a prediction in their notebooks: 'Does the siren itself change its note as it passes, or does something else cause the change we hear? Sketch what you think the sound waves look like in front of and behind the ambulance.' They share with a partner for 2 minutes, then a few share with the class.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'On the way back from Hell's Gate, an ambulance passed us on Waiyaki Way. Kiprono, you were sitting by the window. What did you notice about the siren?' Ask: 'The driver says the siren always makes 700 Hz. So why did we hear different notes?' Give WAIT TIME of 10-15 seconds before taking answers. Then say: 'Write your prediction and draw the waves in front of and behind the ambulance.' Cold-call 4 learners, including at least one who has not spoken today, to read their predictions. Record all ideas on the board without judging, for example 'siren changes', 'wind', 'waves squeezed'.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Predict-Observe-Explain (prediction stage): committing to a prediction in writing and drawing surfaces prior ideas, such as the siren really changes its note, so that later evidence can confirm or challenge them. Linking to the echo phenomenon shows that learners already know sound is a wave that travels and returns.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Look for: (1) learners who draw evenly spaced waves on both sides, showing the common misconception; (2) learners who mention the ambulance moving or waves bunching, which shows readiness; (3) learners who confuse pitch with loudness, since the siren gets louder as it approaches. Tally the number of learners in each group on the board. Use this to decide how much support to give during modelling."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Observe Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Learners watch a short YouTube Doppler demonstration (a siren or moving sound source passing a stationary observer, with wavefront animation). Before it plays, groups of four are given a recording sheet with three columns: 'Approaching', 'Passing', 'Receding'. They record what happens to the pitch and what happens to the spacing of the wavefronts in the animation. Then they study the ambulance data in a table: emitted 700 Hz, approaching 744 Hz, receding 661 Hz, speed 20 m/s. Learners calculate the wavelength of the siren sound at rest using v = f x wavelength: 340/700 = 0.486 m, and notice that the observed frequencies are above and below 700 Hz.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'While you watch, focus only on two things: what the pitch does and how the wavefronts are spaced. Do not write anything until the clip ends.' Play the clip twice. After the second viewing give WAIT TIME of 10 seconds for silent recording. Say: 'Turn to your group. Compare your columns. Where did the wavefronts look closer together?' Circulate and ask each group: 'Which is higher, 744 Hz or 700 Hz? Is that approaching or receding?' Then project the data table and ask: 'Using v = f x wavelength, what is the wavelength of the 700 Hz siren when the ambulance is parked?' Cold-call 3 learners for the working. Ask: 'If the waves are squeezed closer together while the speed of sound stays 340 m/s, what must happen to the frequency?'",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Structured evidence table with Think-Pair-Share: recording observations in fixed categories (approaching, passing, receding) helps learners separate what they see and hear from what they infer. Pairing the animation with numerical data (700, 744, 661 Hz) builds the link between the visual wavefront model and measured frequency.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Check recording sheets for: approaching = higher pitch with wavefronts closer together; receding = lower pitch with wavefronts further apart. Check that learners compute 340/700 = 0.486 m correctly and state that 744 Hz is greater than 700 Hz. Thumbs-up check: 'Does the siren itself change?' Expect most learners to say no. Note groups that still say the ambulance changes its siren."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Explain Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Learners build the explanation in two steps. First, a human-model walk-through in an open space: one learner (Amina) acts as the siren and walks steadily while clapping once per second. A second learner (Otieno) stands still ahead as the observer and a third (Wanjiru) stands behind. Other learners count claps heard per ten seconds at each position. The class sees that Otieno receives claps more often and Wanjiru less often, because each clap starts from a position closer to or further from them. Second, groups draw wavefronts from a moving source on paper: circles starting from successive positions, bunched up in front and stretched behind. They then use the data: approaching 700 x 340/(340-20) = 744 Hz, receding 700 x 340/(340+20) = 661 Hz. They write a claim-evidence-reasoning statement explaining why pitch changes although the siren emits 700 Hz. Finally, Mr. Mwangi introduces uses: a radar speed gun sends radio waves at a moving matatu and measures the shifted frequency of the reflected wave to find its speed, and astronomers see light from distant galaxies shifted to lower frequency (redshift) when they move away.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Amina is our siren. Amina, walk slowly and clap once every second. Everyone else, count claps from where you stand.' After the walk give WAIT TIME of 10-15 seconds, then ask: 'Otieno, in front of Amina, did you get more claps or fewer than Wanjiru behind? Why?' Cold-call 4 learners to explain in their own words. Say: 'Draw circle one where the siren was at time zero, circle two where it was one second later. What happens to the gap in front?' Circulate and prompt: 'Remember, the speed of sound is still 340 m/s. If waves arrive closer together, what does this do to frequency?' Show the two calculations and ask: 'Why is the bottom number 340 minus 20 for approaching and 340 plus 20 for receding?' Wait 10 seconds. Link to the phenomenon: 'Now think of the echo from the Hell's Gate cliff. If a bus was driving towards the cliff while hooting, what would the returning echo sound like?' Cold-call 2 learners. For radar say: 'The radar gun does the same job as the echo: it sends a wave, receives it back, and compares frequency instead of time.' Correct misconceptions: pitch changes are not the same as loudness changes.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Embodied modelling followed by Claim-Evidence-Reasoning: acting out the moving source makes the abstract idea of crowded wavefronts concrete, and the CER structure forces learners to connect the claim (siren appears higher approaching) to evidence (744 Hz and 661 Hz versus 700 Hz) and to the reasoning from v = f x wavelength. Applying the idea to radar and redshift transfers the model to new situations.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Look for CER statements that include: claim (observed frequency depends on relative motion), evidence (744, 700 and 661 Hz), and reasoning (wavefronts bunched ahead and stretched behind, speed of sound unchanged). Check wavefront drawings for closer circles in front of the source and wider circles behind. Check calculations: 700 x 340/320 = 744 Hz and 700 x 340/360 = 661 Hz. Success criterion: at least 80 percent of groups state that the siren does not change, but the number of waves reaching the ear per second does. Note learners who say the speed of sound changes, and revisit v = 340 m/s."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Driving Question Board (DQB) Creation",
        "learnerExperience": "Learners return to the class Driving Question Board. Groups check which earlier questions the Doppler evidence helps with, for example 'what happens to a wave when it meets a moving surface?' They write new questions on sticky notes such as 'Does an echo from a moving wall change pitch?', 'How does a speed gun on a Nairobi highway work?', 'How do we know stars are moving away?' They place notes on the board under categories (reflection, frequency, applications) and mark any older question they can now partly answer.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Look at our Driving Question Board. Which question from earlier lessons does the Doppler effect help with, even a little?' Give WAIT TIME of 10-15 seconds. Then: 'Write one new question on a sticky note and one thing we still do not understand about echoes at the four sites.' Cold-call 3 groups to read notes aloud and place them. Ask: 'Does Doppler explain why Kisumu hall is muddy?' Guide: 'No, that was overlapping reflections. Doppler is about motion, not overlap. So what is different?' Prompt Kiprono's group to link to timing: 'How is the echo delay different from the Doppler pitch change?'",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Driving Question Board questioning: public tracking of what is answered and what is new keeps the whole unit connected to the anchoring phenomenon and shows learners that science grows through questions. Sorting questions by theme builds the habit of separating different wave behaviours.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Check that each group contributes at least one new question and can say which category it belongs to. Look for questions that link Doppler to echoes (moving reflector, moving observer) as a sign of deeper connection. Note learners who place Doppler questions under 'interference' and clarify in the next lesson."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Model Building Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Learners revise the class echo model. Amina leads a group in adding a new Doppler panel: a moving source with bunched wavefronts ahead and stretched wavefronts behind, labelled 'approaching: higher f, shorter wavelength; receding: lower f, longer wavelength'. Each group adds a note to the existing four-site echo model (Hell's Gate 0.50 s, Ngong 1.00 s, Kisumu hall 0.07 s, Nairobi grounds no echo): 'A moving reflector or source changes the pitch of what returns; speed of sound stays 340 m/s.' Learners then complete the summary table prompts in their notebooks: what we observed, what we learned, how it helps explain echoes.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Amina, bring your wavefront diagram to the board. Everyone else, tell us what must be added to our class model.' Give WAIT TIME of 10-15 seconds. Ask: 'Which parts of our echo model stay the same and which parts are new?' Cold-call 4 learners: one to state the Doppler rule, one to give the 744 Hz and 661 Hz values, one to name a use in radar, and one to name a use in astronomy. Say: 'At Hell's Gate the cliff does not move, so the echo has the same pitch as the shout. What would change if the cliff or the shouter moved?' Close with: 'Next lesson we will see how waves are used in FM radio and ultrasound, which both depend on frequency and on echo timing.'",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Cumulative model revision: adding a Doppler panel to the class model shows that the same wave ideas (v = f x wavelength, pitch, reflection) explain different behaviours. Explicitly stating what stays the same in the Hell's Gate echo and what would change with motion helps learners transfer the model.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Check that the revised model shows wavefront spacing with labelled higher and lower frequency and that the speed of sound is kept at 340 m/s. Exit check: each learner writes one sentence beginning 'The siren seems higher when approaching because...' and one use of the Doppler effect. Success indicator: sentences mention waves reaching the observer more often, not that the siren changed."
      }
    ],
    "teacherReflection": "Which learners still believe the siren itself changes its note, and what evidence from the wavefront drawings or the human-model walk helped them change their mind? Did learners keep the speed of sound at 340 m/s while explaining frequency change? Was the claim-evidence-reasoning structure used with the numbers 700, 744 and 661 Hz? Which DQB questions need to be carried into Lesson 11 on communication and medicine?",
    "summaryTablePrompt": {
      "observed": "We watched and acted out a moving sound source. The pitch of the ambulance siren sounded higher as it approached (744 Hz) and lower as it receded (661 Hz), although the siren emits 700 Hz.",
      "learned": "Relative motion of source and observer changes the observed frequency: waves bunch up ahead of a moving source (higher frequency) and stretch out behind it (lower frequency). The speed of sound stays 340 m/s and v = f x wavelength still holds. This is used in radar to measure speed and in astronomy to tell whether stars and galaxies are moving towards or away from us.",
      "explained": "At Hell's Gate and Ngong Hills the sound returns from a fixed cliff, so its pitch matches the shout and only the delay (0.50 s and 1.00 s) tells us the distance. If the source, observer or reflecting surface were moving, the returned sound would also change in frequency, which is how radar guns find the speed of a matatu. Doppler explains pitch change, not why the Kisumu hall is muddy or why the Nairobi grounds have no echo."
    }
  },
  {
    "number": 11,
    "title": "Waves in Communication and Medicine: FM Radio and Ultrasound as Echo Applications",
    "duration": "40 minutes (double period 80 minutes recommended if the ultrasound timing task is completed in class)",
    "substrand": "Sub-Strand 2.1: Properties of Waves",
    "aresKeywords": "FM radio, carrier frequency, frequency modulation, radio waves, electromagnetic wave, ultrasound, echo timing, d = vt/2, pulse-echo, tissue boundary, wavelength, v = f x wavelength",
    "slo": {
      "purpose": "Learners apply what they know about echoes and frequency to two real technologies. They explain how an FM radio station carries a voice by changing the frequency of a radio carrier wave. They then show how an ultrasound scanner at a county hospital finds the depth of an organ boundary by timing a reflected pulse, in the same way that learners at Hell's Gate timed their echo. Both applications show that the wave behaviours in this unit are used in everyday life.",
      "knowledge": "By the end of the lesson the learner should be able to: (a) state that radio waves are electromagnetic waves that travel at 3.0 x 10^8 m/s and need no medium; (b) calculate the wavelength of the 91.0 MHz FM carrier using v = f x wavelength (3.30 m); (c) explain that in FM the sound signal changes the frequency of the carrier, not its amplitude; (d) explain that ultrasound is sound above the range of human hearing, and that a 3.5 MHz probe in soft tissue (1540 m/s) has a wavelength of 0.44 mm; (e) use d = vt/2 to relate echo time to boundary depth, including the 7.7 cm boundary returning an echo after 100 microseconds.",
      "skills": "NGSS Science and Engineering Practices: Developing and Using Models (draw a labelled model of an FM carrier and of a pulse-echo scan); Using Mathematics and Computational Thinking (apply v = f x wavelength and d = vt/2 with correct units and powers of ten); Analyzing and Interpreting Data (compare Hell's Gate echo timing with ultrasound timing); Constructing Explanations (link technology to the echo phenomenon); Obtaining, Evaluating and Communicating Information (use the Khan Academy resource on waves applications and communicate findings to peers).",
      "attitudes": "Core competencies: Digital literacy (learners use the Khan Academy resource on radio and FM modulation); Critical thinking and problem solving (learners reason from echo timing to depth); Communication and collaboration (learners explain to a partner and the class). Values: Responsibility (handling devices and the offline resources carefully, and taking turns); Respect (listening to peers' explanations). PCI: Health education (learners appreciate that ultrasound is a safe imaging method used in county hospitals, for example in antenatal care).",
      "keyInquiry": "1. How do waves behave when they meet obstacles or change medium? 2. How are the properties of waves applied in day-to-day life?",
      "purposeInStoryline": "Lessons 1 to 10 gathered evidence that sound reflects, that echo delay follows t = 2d/v, and that frequency and wavelength are linked by v = f x wavelength. In Lesson 11 learners see that the same ideas run radio and medical imaging: an ultrasound scanner is a Hell's Gate echo on a tiny scale, and an FM station uses frequency, the property studied in the Doppler lesson, to carry a voice. The lesson adds two entries to the DQB and the class model and prepares learners for Lesson 12, where they bring all wave behaviours together to give the final explanation of the four echo sites and examine noise.",
      "safetyNotes": "Keep all sound levels from phones, radios and speakers below the 85 dB class limit, and keep the volume moderate when the FM radio is played. Do not use any real ultrasound device on learners, because the scanner is studied only through data and diagrams. Handle phones, radios and batteries carefully, and keep electrical devices away from water. Learners should not put earphones in at high volume."
    },
    "overview": "Wanjiru asks Mr. Mwangi, 'If sound needs air to travel, how does my voice get from the radio station in Nairobi to the radio in Kericho?' The lesson uses this question to link the Hell's Gate echo to two technologies. In the Predict Phase learners predict how a voice travels to an FM radio and how a doctor could see an organ with sound. In the Observe Phase they use the Khan Academy resource on waves applications (radio, radar, Doppler) and a short data task to see how an FM carrier at 91.0 MHz has a wavelength of 3.30 m and how a 3.5 MHz ultrasound pulse returns from a boundary 7.7 cm deep after 100 microseconds. In the Explain Phase they use d = vt/2 and v = f x wavelength to explain both technologies and compare them to the echo timing of Hell's Gate (0.50 s) and Ngong Hills (1.00 s). They then add questions to the DQB and extend the class model with a 'technology' branch showing that the same wave ideas work at very different scales.",
    "framework": [
      {
        "phase": "Predict Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Learners sit in groups of four. Mr. Mwangi tells the story: Wanjiru listens to an FM station at 91.0 MHz in Kericho while Kiprono's mother has an ultrasound scan at a county hospital. Wanjiru asks, 'Is the radio using echoes like our shouting at Hell's Gate?' Individually, learners write and sketch predictions for two questions. (1) How does a voice from a studio in Nairobi reach a radio far away, and what do you think a 'station frequency' of 91.0 MHz means? (2) How can a doctor use sound that we cannot hear to find where an organ boundary is inside the body? Learners write one sentence for each and mark how sure they are (1 to 3). They then share with a partner and give a possible link to the Hell's Gate echo (85 m, 0.50 s).",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Today we leave the cliffs and walk into a radio studio and a hospital. Before I teach anything, I want your best thinking.' Display the two questions. Give 3 minutes of silent writing. WAIT TIME: 10 to 15 seconds of silence after posing each question before any talking. Say: 'Wanjiru's radio plays a voice that was in a studio kilometres away. Does the sound travel through the air all the way? Write what you think.' Cold-call 4 learners (two per question) with: 'Tell us your prediction and one reason, even if you are not sure.' Record predictions on a chart under the headings 'Radio' and 'Ultrasound'. Do not correct. Say: 'I notice many of you think the voice itself travels as sound. We will test that today.' Ask: 'What do the shout at Hell's Gate and a hospital scan have in common?' Cold-call 2 more learners and note any who mention reflection or delay.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Predict-Observe-Explain (POE) with a Prediction Chart: Writing and sharing predictions first surfaces the common misconception that radio carries ordinary sound through air, and the idea that sound at Hell's Gate cannot go through the body. Learners have a record to compare their later evidence with, which makes the change in their thinking visible and gives them a reason to want to explain.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Look for: (1) every learner has written a prediction and a confidence rating; (2) at least 6 of the learners can name a link to echo or reflection; (3) the misconceptions to monitor are 'radio waves are sound waves' and 'ultrasound is just very loud sound'. Tally them on the board. Use a quick show of fingers (1 = not sure, 3 = very sure) to gauge confidence and record the number of 3s to revisit at the end of the lesson."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Observe Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Part A (about 8 minutes): FM radio. Learners use the Khan Academy resource on waves applications (radio, radar, Doppler), or the printed offline extract, and the teacher's FM radio tuned to a station at moderate volume. Task: (1) record that radio waves are electromagnetic waves, travelling at 3.0 x 10^8 m/s, and need no medium (build on Lessons 2 and 5); (2) calculate the wavelength of the 91.0 MHz carrier: lambda = v/f = 3.0 x 10^8 / 91.0 x 10^6 = 3.30 m; (3) look at the drawn graphs of a carrier wave and a voice signal and note that in FM the carrier's frequency is squeezed and stretched by the voice while the height (amplitude) stays the same. Part B (about 10 minutes): Ultrasound. Learners look at the pulse-echo diagram and data card: probe 3.5 MHz, speed in soft tissue 1540 m/s. They calculate the wavelength: 1540 / 3.5 x 10^6 = 0.00044 m (0.44 mm). They then use d = vt/2 to find the time for an echo from a boundary 7.7 cm (0.077 m) deep: t = 2 x 0.077 / 1540 = 1.0 x 10^-4 s (100 microseconds). Groups complete a comparison table of Hell's Gate (85 m, 0.50 s), Ngong Hills (170 m, 1.00 s) and the 7.7 cm boundary (100 microseconds), recording the speed, the distance, the time and what is reflecting.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Scientists, you are data collectors now. Write down numbers with units.' Hold up the FM radio and say: 'This station is 91.0 MHz. M means mega, which is one million hertz. How many waves pass each second?' WAIT TIME: 10 to 15 seconds before taking answers. Circulate and ask: 'Which speed do you use for radio, 340 m/s or 3.0 x 10^8 m/s? Why?' Cold-call 3 learners. Point to the carrier graph: 'What stays the same here, and what changes?' Cold-call 2 learners. In Part B ask: 'Why do we divide by 2 in d = vt/2 just like at Hell's Gate?' WAIT TIME: 10 to 15 seconds. Cold-call 3 learners; look for 'the pulse goes to the boundary and back'. Check powers of ten for groups struggling with 10^6 and 10^-4: 'Show me how you entered 3.5 x 10^6 on your calculator.' Remind learners that the ultrasound speed is 1540 m/s, not 340 m/s. Do not explain the full modulation yet; let the data speak.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Data table comparison and Claim-Evidence notes: Putting three echo situations (a cliff, a valley and an organ boundary) in one table makes learners see that the same relation d = vt/2 works across very different scales, and that only the wave speed and the time change. Recording numbers and units first, before explanation, trains learners to base claims on evidence.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Check the worksheets for: wavelength 3.30 m written with units; wavelength 0.44 mm (or 0.00044 m); t = 100 microseconds (1.0 x 10^-4 s); use of 1540 m/s in tissue and 3.0 x 10^8 m/s for radio; the statement that in FM the carrier frequency changes while amplitude stays constant. Observable indicator: at least 3 of the 4 groups correctly fill in the table row for the 7.7 cm boundary. Note the groups that divide by 2 correctly and those that forget. Ask any group that has not finished to explain the first row verbally."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Explain Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Learners meet in their groups of four with the four character roles: Wanjiru (asks questions), Otieno (checks the evidence), Kiprono (checks the timing and calculations) and Amina (draws the model). Each group writes a short Claim-Evidence-Reasoning explanation for two statements. (1) 'An FM station sends a voice by changing the frequency of a carrier wave, and the radio wave does not need air.' (2) 'An ultrasound scanner finds the depth of a tissue boundary using the same echo idea as the Hell's Gate cliff.' They explain why the 7.7 cm echo returns in 100 microseconds when the Hell's Gate echo returns in 0.50 s, using the speeds and distances in their table. They also answer the Lesson 10 link: the Doppler lesson showed frequency changing with motion, and FM shows frequency changing because the sound signal changes it. Finally, each group answers: why can a doctor not use the Hell's Gate shout to scan a body? (The wavelength of audible sound is too long to show small detail, and ultrasound has a wavelength of 0.44 mm, a size suited to small boundaries.) Groups share their reasoning with another group.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Use evidence from your table. A claim is not enough, I want a number and a reason.' Display the sentence frames: 'I claim that ... because the data show ... which means ...'. Move among groups and ask: 'If the carrier frequency is 91.0 MHz and I speak into the microphone, what exactly changes in the wave?' WAIT TIME: 10 to 15 seconds. Cold-call 3 groups to share; ask the others to agree or disagree and say why. Ask: 'What is the same between the cliff at Hell's Gate and the organ boundary? What is different?' Cold-call 4 learners. Push with: 'Which wave needs a medium, which does not, and how do we know?' Cold-call 2 learners and connect to Lessons 2 and 5 (electromagnetic waves, light needing no medium). Correct only after learners have tried: radio is not sound; the voice is converted to an electrical signal that changes the carrier frequency, and a radio converts it back to sound. Ask: 'Why does the scanner need to send short pulses and then wait?' WAIT TIME: 10 to 15 seconds, so learners link this to the 0.1 s echo idea (separate pulses and echoes). Close with: 'So what do the cliff, the Kisumu hall, the radio and the scanner have in common?'",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Claim-Evidence-Reasoning (CER) with peer critique: Building a claim from data and then reasoning with wave ideas (echo timing, frequency, medium) pushes learners to connect the technology to the same physics as the phenomenon. Peer critique makes weak reasoning visible, such as 'radio is just loud sound', and lets learners repair it themselves before the teacher confirms the science.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Listen for and record: (1) learners say 'frequency of the carrier changes, amplitude stays the same' in FM; (2) learners state that radio waves are electromagnetic and need no medium; (3) learners explain 'divide by 2 because the pulse goes there and back'; (4) learners connect the short wavelength (0.44 mm) to seeing small detail. Success criterion: at least 75% of groups give a CER that includes a number, a unit and a link to the echo or frequency idea. Mark learners who still confuse radio waves and sound waves for a short follow-up conversation before the next lesson."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Driving Question Board (DQB) Creation",
        "learnerExperience": "Learners return to the DQB (How do sound waves reflect, and why do some places produce stronger echoes than others?). Each group writes on sticky notes: (a) one question that this lesson has answered (for example, 'How is the echo idea used in medicine?') and sticks it in the 'answered' area with the evidence; (b) one new question that has appeared (for example, 'Could a hall be designed so that the muddy echo disappears?', 'Does ultrasound reflect more from some tissues than others like the surface table?', 'Why do radios in the Rift Valley sometimes lose a station?'). They place the notes and then read the notes of other groups. The class sorts them into the headings 'Reflection', 'Frequency and waves', 'Applications', and 'Noise and design for Lesson 12'.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Our board is a record of our thinking. Today, what can we move from questions to answers?' Give 4 minutes to write. Ask: 'Which old question does the ultrasound example help answer? Show me the evidence.' WAIT TIME: 10 to 15 seconds. Cold-call 4 groups to place a note and read it. Ask: 'Does ultrasound reflect equally from every tissue? What did the surface table from Lesson 4 say about different surfaces?' Cold-call 2 learners. Point at the question on the muddy echo in the Kisumu hall: 'Which of today's ideas can help? We will look at that in Lesson 12.' Do not answer new questions, but mark them as 'to investigate'. Say: 'Wanjiru, which of your questions has changed since Hell's Gate?' to model that questions evolve.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Driving Question Board (DQB) tracking and sorting: Writing and categorising their own questions gives learners ownership of the unit and shows how each lesson fits the larger puzzle. Moving questions to 'answered' with evidence shows learning progress, and the unanswered ones guide the next investigation.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Count and review the notes: at least 4 new questions from different groups; at least 2 notes moved to 'answered' with a cited piece of evidence (for example, 'the 100 microsecond calculation'); at least 1 question that links radio or ultrasound back to echoes or reflection from different surfaces. Look for questions that use the key terms (frequency, wavelength, reflection, medium). Note any group that only copies earlier questions and prompt them with 'What puzzled you today?'."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Model Building Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Amina leads each group in revising the class model of the echo. Groups add a 'Technology' branch to the model with two labelled sketches. Sketch 1: a radio station tower sending a carrier at 91.0 MHz (wavelength 3.30 m) with a note 'voice changes the frequency, not the height; radio waves do not need a medium; speed 3.0 x 10^8 m/s'. Sketch 2: an ultrasound probe (3.5 MHz, wavelength 0.44 mm, speed 1540 m/s) sending a pulse to a boundary 7.7 cm deep and receiving an echo after 100 microseconds, using d = vt/2. Each sketch has an arrow back to the central model of Hell's Gate (0.50 s), Ngong Hills (1.00 s), the Kisumu hall (0.07 s, reverberation) and the Nairobi open grounds (no echo). Groups add a sentence: 'The same ideas of reflection, timing and frequency work at every scale.' They complete the summary table (what we observed, what we learned, how we explain it) and note one thing in the model still unexplained, to be handled in Lesson 12.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Models are tools to explain and predict. Make sure every arrow and label is evidence-based.' Display the class model and ask: 'Where does the ultrasound pulse go, and where does it come back from? Point on your sketch.' WAIT TIME: 10 to 15 seconds. Cold-call 3 groups to explain a sketch to the class. Ask: 'Does the ultrasound echo model use the same formula as the cliff? What changes?' Cold-call 2 learners (speed and time). Ask: 'Which part of the model explains why the Kisumu hall is muddy, and which part do we still need?' Cold-call 2 learners and record 'Lesson 12: noise and absorbing treatment'. Check that radio waves are not drawn as sound and the FM sketch shows the frequency changing. Finish with an exit slip: 'Write one similarity and one difference between the Hell's Gate echo and an ultrasound echo.' Collect the slips.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Cumulative Model Building and Revision: Extending the class echo model with technology branches asks learners to apply the same mechanism to new contexts, which shows transfer of understanding. Labelled arrows to the four sites force them to connect the wave ideas to the original phenomenon, and the 'still unexplained' note maintains a purpose for the final lesson.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Check each model for: (1) FM sketch showing frequency variation with constant amplitude, the 3.30 m wavelength and no medium; (2) ultrasound sketch with the 7.7 cm boundary, 1540 m/s and 100 microseconds; (3) d = vt/2 written with 'divide by 2 because there and back'; (4) at least one arrow linking back to a Hell's Gate, Ngong, Kisumu or Nairobi site. Exit slip success: at least 80% of learners state a correct similarity (both use reflection and timing, d = vt/2) and a correct difference (speed, scale or wave type). Learners who miss these get a short re-teach at the start of Lesson 12."
      }
    ],
    "teacherReflection": "After the lesson ask yourself: Did learners distinguish radio waves (electromagnetic, no medium, 3.0 x 10^8 m/s) from sound waves (mechanical, need a medium, 340 m/s in air and 1540 m/s in tissue)? Which learners still say that radio carries the sound itself? Could they use d = vt/2 correctly, including the division by 2 and the powers of ten for 100 microseconds? Did the table linking Hell's Gate, Ngong Hills and the 7.7 cm boundary help them see the same idea at different scales? Which groups needed the most prompting to give reasoning rather than only a claim? Did the DQB questions show curiosity about the Kisumu hall, which will be useful in Lesson 12? Note adjustments for pacing (the calculations with powers of ten may need extra time) and plan support for learners who need it before the final lesson.",
    "summaryTablePrompt": {
      "observed": "We observed that the 91.0 MHz FM carrier is a radio wave of wavelength 3.30 m travelling at 3.0 x 10^8 m/s, and that in FM the voice changes the frequency of the carrier while its amplitude stays constant. We observed that a 3.5 MHz ultrasound pulse in soft tissue (1540 m/s) has a wavelength of 0.44 mm and that an organ boundary 7.7 cm deep returns an echo after 100 microseconds.",
      "learned": "We learned that radio waves are electromagnetic waves that need no medium, that FM carries sound by changing the carrier frequency, and that ultrasound imaging uses reflected pulses and echo timing with d = vt/2 to locate tissue boundaries. We learned that the echo idea from Hell's Gate (0.50 s) and Ngong Hills (1.00 s) works at both large and tiny scales.",
      "explained": "We explain that the same wave ideas explain the phenomenon and the technologies: a wave travels, meets a boundary, reflects, and returns after a time equal to 2d/v, so the delay tells us the distance. In the radio, the frequency of the wave carries the information. This shows how wave properties are applied in day-to-day life, and we will use all these ideas in Lesson 12 to give the final explanation of the four echo sites."
    }
  },
  {
    "number": 12,
    "title": "Noise Pollution and the Final Model: Why Do Some Places Echo and Others Do Not?",
    "duration": "80 minutes (2 x 40-minute periods)",
    "substrand": "Sub-Strand 2.1: Properties of Waves",
    "aresKeywords": "noise pollution, decibel (dB), echo, reverberation, absorption, reflection, interference, resonance, final model, Driving Question Board",
    "slo": {
      "purpose": "Learners analyse sources and health effects of noise, design noise-reduction treatments for the Kisumu hall, and use all wave ideas from Lessons 1 to 11 to build and present a final model explaining echoes at Hell's Gate, Ngong Hills, the Kisumu hall and the Nairobi open grounds.",
      "knowledge": "Noise levels in decibels (whisper 30 dB, quiet classroom 50 dB, conversation 60 dB, Kisumu hall during assembly 85 dB, matatu with loud music 95 dB; safe limit used in class 85 dB for up to 8 hours). Hard smooth surfaces reflect most sound energy (cliff rock 90%, painted concrete 85%, wooden board 60%) while soft surfaces absorb it (curtains 15%, thick grass or foam mattress 10% reflected). Echo means a distinct reflected sound at least 0.1 s later (reflector at least 17 m away), and d = vt/2 with v = 340 m/s. Reverberation means overlapping reflections that merge and prolong the sound. All four echo sites A to D can be explained by reflection, delay, distance and absorption. Other wave behaviours (refraction, diffraction, interference, resonance, Doppler) follow the same wave ideas.",
      "skills": "Developing and using models (NGSS SEP 2): compare the Lesson 1 model with the final model. Constructing explanations (SEP 6): write a claim-evidence-reasoning final explanation. Analysing and interpreting data (SEP 4): use the decibel list, echo table and surface table. Designing solutions (SEP 6 engineering): propose absorbing treatment for the Kisumu hall. Communication and collaboration: present and critique models.",
      "attitudes": "Responsibility: care for hearing health and the community's noise environment. Respect: listen to and fairly critique peers' models and designs. Curiosity and appreciation of the usefulness of Physics in day-to-day life.",
      "keyInquiry": "1. How do waves behave when they meet obstacles or change medium? 2. How are the properties of waves applied in day-to-day life?",
      "purposeInStoryline": "This is the final lesson. Learners apply what they learned across Lessons 1 to 11, especially echo distance and surfaces (Lesson 4), interference (Lesson 8), resonance (Lesson 9) and reflection (Lesson 3), to a real problem (noise in the Kisumu hall). They then close the Driving Question Board (DQB) and compare their Lesson 1 first model with a final model that fully answers: How do sound waves reflect, and why do some places produce stronger echoes than others?",
      "safetyNotes": "Do not play loud sounds in class; use a phone or speaker at normal volume only. If a sound level app is used, measure only ordinary classroom sounds, never produce loud noise on purpose. Keep movement around the room orderly while learners post cards on the DQB and carry foam, cloth and wooden board samples."
    },
    "overview": "Mr. Mwangi opens with Otieno's question from the trip: why was the Kisumu hall so loud and muddy when it was only 12 m from the far wall? Learners first predict which noise sources are dangerous using the decibel list, then analyse sources and health effects, test how much different materials absorb sound (using the surface table: cliff rock 90%, painted concrete 85%, wooden board 60%, curtains 15%, foam or grass 10% reflected) and design an absorbing treatment for the Kisumu hall. In the second half, learners revisit the Driving Question Board, sort evidence into answered, partly answered and new questions, and build a final model of all four echo sites (A Hell's Gate 0.50 s, B Ngong 1.00 s, C Kisumu 0.07 s with about 1.2 s reverberation, D Nairobi 1.76 s but too faint). Amina's Lesson 1 model is compared with the final model, and every learner writes a Final Explanation using claim, evidence and reasoning. The Final Explanation: waves are disturbances that transfer energy without transferring matter; sound reflects from hard smooth surfaces following the law of reflection, returns after t = 2d/v, and is heard as an echo only when the reflector is at least 17 m away and reflects enough energy.",
    "framework": [
      {
        "phase": "Predict Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Learners look at five cards showing: whisper, quiet classroom, ordinary conversation, Kisumu hall during assembly, and matatu with loud music. Individually, in their notebooks, they predict (a) the order from quietest to loudest, (b) which sounds could damage hearing, and (c) why the Kisumu hall during assembly is so loud even though it is a school. They write a number guess in dB for each card without looking at any list. Then in pairs they predict: 'If we hung curtains and put foam on the Kisumu hall walls, what would happen to the muddy sound and why?' Predictions are connected to Otieno's observation at the Kisumu hall (site C) where the voice was loud, muddy and drawn out.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Remember Otieno in the Kisumu hall? The wall was only 12 m away, yet the sound was loud and muddy. Today we decide how to fix that and then finish our big answer.' Hand out five cards and say: 'Rank them and guess the decibels. Do not talk yet, write for 3 minutes.' After writing, WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds before asking for any answer. Cold-call 4 learners with: 'Which source do you think is most harmful? What is your reason?' Cold-call 2 more: 'What will curtains and foam do to the sound in the hall?' Record the predictions on the board without judging. Ask: 'What do you already know from Lesson 4 about hard and soft surfaces that could help you predict?' WAIT TIME 10 seconds.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Predict-Observe-Explain (POE) with individual commitment: writing a prediction before seeing data makes learners' prior ideas visible, so they notice later where evidence confirms or changes their thinking. Linking the prediction to site C connects noise to reflection and reverberation from earlier lessons.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Observe whether learners (1) rank matatu with loud music above conversation, (2) mention loudness or amplitude and not only pitch, (3) say that soft materials will reduce the reflected sound. Tally on a clipboard how many learners predict that soft materials absorb sound (target: more than half). Note the misconception that thicker walls always stop noise, or that echoes and noise are the same thing, for follow-up in the Explain Phase."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Observe Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Part 1 (decibel data): In groups of four (one Wanjiru-style questioner, one Kiprono-style timekeeper, one Amina-style recorder, one Otieno-style reporter), learners get the decibel list: whisper 30 dB, quiet classroom 50 dB, ordinary conversation 60 dB, Kisumu hall during assembly 85 dB, matatu with loud music 95 dB, class safe limit 85 dB for up to 8 hours. They draw a bar chart, mark the 85 dB line, identify which sources are at or above it, and list noise sources they meet daily (matatus along Kisumu roads, market loudspeakers, generators, boda boda horns, school assemblies). They read a short card on health effects: hearing damage when exposure is above the limit, poor concentration, headaches, stress and disturbed sleep. Part 2 (absorption investigation): Using the surface table (approximate share of sound energy reflected), groups examine samples (wooden board, cloth or curtain, foam mattress piece, and a smooth painted board or wall surface). They hold a phone playing a steady ordinary-volume tone or a ticking clock near each sample at the same distance, listen to or measure the sound reflected, and rank the samples from strongest reflection to weakest. They compare their ranking with the table values: cliff rock 90%, painted concrete 85%, wooden board 60%, curtains/cloth 15%, thick grass/foam mattress 10%. Part 3 (calculation link): groups recompute for site C: round trip 24 m, t = 2d/v = 24/340 = 0.07 s, which is less than 0.1 s, so the echo merges with the voice, and reflections from hard walls and roof keep the sound lingering about 1.2 s (reverberation).",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'You are acoustic engineers for a Kisumu school. First, let us read the noise data, then test materials.' Circulate and ask each group: 'Which source is above the 85 dB limit? What does that mean for a matatu driver or a conductor who works all day?' Push for the idea that dose depends on loudness and time: 'Why is 85 dB allowed for 8 hours but 95 dB is risky?' WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds after each question. For the absorption test, say: 'Keep the distance and volume the same. Why is that important?' (fair test, control variables). Cold-call 3 groups to report their ranking: 'Which material reflected most? Which least? What evidence did you hear or measure?' Then ask: 'In site C, why is a 0.07 s delay not heard as a separate echo?' WAIT TIME 10 seconds, cold-call 2 learners. Remind learners to keep all sounds at ordinary classroom volume.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Data analysis with a fair-test investigation (SEP 3 and 4): learners combine numerical decibel data with a hands-on comparison of materials, then check their results against the surface table. Comparing hard and soft surfaces turns the abstract idea of 'percentage of energy reflected' into something they can hear.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Check that bar charts have the 85 dB line and correctly identify the Kisumu hall (85 dB, at the limit) and matatu with loud music (95 dB, above the limit) as hazards. Look for groups that keep distance and volume constant. Listen for explanations that use the idea of energy being absorbed (not just 'cloth blocks sound'). Check that learners calculate 0.07 s correctly and state that it is less than 0.1 s. Indicator of success: at least 80% of groups rank wooden board above cloth and foam in reflection, matching the surface table."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Explain Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Learners design a noise-reduction treatment for the Kisumu hall in their groups. They must (1) state the problem using evidence (12 m to the far wall, 0.07 s delay, reverberation about 1.2 s, 85 dB at assembly), (2) choose two or three materials and where to place them (curtains at windows, foam or acoustic panels on the back wall, a carpet or mats on the floor, wooden panels or ceiling tiles), (3) justify each choice using the surface table (for example curtains reflect only 15%, foam 10%, compared to painted concrete 85%), (4) explain how the treatment reduces reverberation by reducing repeated reflections and sound energy, and (5) propose one barrier or planting solution for the outside noise from a nearby road (a thick wall, a row of trees and shrubs, or moving the hall windows away from the matatu stage), explaining that barriers reflect or absorb sound before it reaches the hall. They then extend to other wave ideas. Using two sentences each, they link: interference (Lesson 8), since the overlapping reflections in the Kisumu hall meet and add; resonance (Lesson 9), since some hall frequencies can be amplified when the room or objects vibrate at their natural frequency; and reflection (Lesson 3), since the law of reflection explains the direction of the returning sound. Each group presents its design in a 2-minute gallery-style share, and the class gives one star (strength) and one wish (improvement).",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Design like an engineer: every choice needs evidence from our tables.' Provide a design template with the five required parts. Circulate and ask: 'Why curtains and not another wooden board? What percentage of sound does each reflect?' WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds. Ask: 'If you remove 85% reflection from the walls, what happens to the reverberation time of about 1.2 s?' Cold-call 3 groups for their reasoning. Say: 'Absorbing material reduces reverberation. It does not remove the sound from the speaker, so the hall is still audible, only clearer.' Challenge a common misconception: 'Does absorbing material make a place quieter and also remove echo?' Clarify: in a hall of 12 m the echo was never a separate echo (0.07 s), it was reverberation. During the gallery share, cold-call 4 learners from different groups to give a star and a wish. Highlight any design that links absorption to reducing reflected energy. Make links explicit: 'Lesson 8 told us waves overlap and add. Lesson 3 told us reflection follows the law. Lesson 4 told us hard surfaces reflect strongly.'",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Engineering design with Claim-Evidence-Reasoning (CER): learners propose a solution (claim), cite surface and decibel data (evidence) and explain with wave ideas of reflection, absorption and overlap (reasoning). Star-and-wish peer critique builds understanding by asking learners to evaluate other designs against the same evidence.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Use a four-point checklist: (1) problem stated with correct numbers (12 m, 0.07 s, about 1.2 s, 85 dB), (2) materials justified with percentages, (3) reverberation explained as repeated reflections reduced by absorption, (4) at least one link to interference, resonance or reflection. Look for the misconception that absorbing material removes the 0.07 s delay itself. Exit signal: learners hold up 1 to 4 fingers for how many checklist items their design meets. Target: at least 75% show 3 or more."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Driving Question Board (DQB) Creation",
        "learnerExperience": "Final DQB review. The DQB, which has grown from Lesson 1 with questions such as Wanjiru's 'Why does the voice come back?', Otieno's 'Why is the hall echo muddy?' and Kiprono's 'How can we measure the delay?', is revisited. Groups receive their question cards and sort them into three columns: Answered (with evidence and lesson number), Partly Answered, and New Questions. Examples: 'Why is there a delay?' is answered by Lesson 4: t = 2d/v, so site A is 170/340 = 0.50 s and site B is 340/340 = 1.00 s. 'Why no echo at Nairobi grounds (site D)?' is answered by spreading, absorption by grass and bushes, and the 300 m wall giving a 1.76 s delay but a very faint sound. 'Why does sound turn corners but light does not?' is answered by diffraction (Lesson 7). Each learner writes one new question for further inquiry, such as 'How do bats use echoes?', 'How does noise-cancelling work in a matatu?' or 'Can hearing damage from noise be reversed?' The class reads the main driving question aloud and decides how fully it is now answered.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Today we close our board. For each question, show the evidence that answers it and name the lesson.' Give each group 8 to 10 cards. WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds before groups begin sorting. Circulate and ask: 'Which lesson gave you this answer? What number or observation supports it?' Cold-call 5 learners to place one card and defend its column. Challenge: 'Which card is still only partly answered, and what evidence is missing?' Ask Wanjiru-type questioners to read their original question and say whether they are satisfied. Ask each learner to add one new question card. Say: 'Good scientists finish with new questions.' Photograph or note the final DQB for the class record.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Question sorting with evidence mapping on the Driving Question Board: linking each question to a specific lesson and data point makes progress visible and shows learners that understanding is built from accumulated evidence. Adding new questions promotes continued inquiry beyond the unit.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Check that learners cite a lesson and a piece of evidence for at least 80% of 'Answered' cards. Listen for accurate use of d = vt/2, the 17 m minimum distance, 0.1 s rule, surface percentages and the difference between echo and reverberation. Note whether new questions are testable and connected to waves (for example about noise control, animals or technology). Look for learners who still place the Kisumu hall under 'echo' rather than 'reverberation' and address this immediately."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Model Building Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Learners build the Final Model and Final Explanation. Step 1: Each group takes Amina's Lesson 1 first model (a simple sketch of a shout going to a cliff and back with the question 'Why does the voice come back?') and lists what it explained and what it missed. Step 2: Groups draw a final model on a large sheet showing all four sites A to D. Each site shows the learner, the straight ray path to the reflector with the angle of incidence i equal to the angle of reflection r measured from the normal, distance d, round trip 2d and the delay t = 2d/340. Site A Hell's Gate: 85 m, 170 m round trip, 0.50 s, clear echo, cliff rock reflects about 90%. Site B Ngong Hills: 170 m, 340 m round trip, 1.00 s, clear echo. Site C Kisumu hall: 12 m, 24 m round trip, 0.07 s, less than 0.1 s, so the echo merges with the voice, and hard walls and roof keep reflecting, giving reverberation of about 1.2 s, loud, muddy and drawn out, with an 85 dB assembly level. Site D Nairobi open grounds: nearest large hard wall (classroom block) 300 m away, 600 m round trip, 1.76 s, but the sound spreads over a huge area and the grass and bushes absorb and scatter it (grass about 10% reflected), so the returning sound is too faint, no echo. Step 3: Each group adds a panel linking other wave behaviours to the same ideas: refraction (waves change direction when speed changes), diffraction (sound with wavelength 1.0 m at 340 Hz spreads through a 1.0 m doorway), interference (path difference 1.0 m gives loud, 0.5 m gives quiet), resonance (nyatiti 100 Hz, flute 500 Hz), Doppler (ambulance 744 Hz approaching, 661 Hz receding) and applications (FM 91.0 MHz with wavelength 3.30 m, ultrasound 3.5 MHz with 100 microsecond echo). Step 4: Gallery walk and comparison of the Lesson 1 model with the final model. Step 5: Each learner writes an individual Final Explanation using claim, evidence and reasoning, beginning: 'Waves are disturbances that transfer energy from one point to another without transferring matter.' It must answer: How do sound waves reflect, and why do some places produce stronger echoes than others? and finish with one real-life use of waves and one way to reduce noise.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Pull out our first model from Lesson 1. What could it not explain?' WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds, then cold-call 3 learners. Give model criteria: (1) all four sites with numbers, (2) the law of reflection with the normal, (3) delay equals 2d/v, (4) the 17 m and 0.1 s condition, (5) surface absorption and spreading, (6) reverberation versus echo, (7) at least four other wave behaviours. While groups work, ask: 'Why is site D silent even though its delay is the longest? Why is site C not an echo even though the walls reflect well?' WAIT TIME 10 seconds. Cold-call 2 learners per question. Challenge: 'Does a longer delay mean a stronger echo?' (No: strength depends on the energy reflected, which depends on the surface and spreading.) Lead the gallery walk: groups leave one star and one question sticky note on two other models. Then bring the class together and ask 5 cold-called learners to state the biggest change between the Lesson 1 model and the final model. Finish with: 'Write your Final Explanation alone, in about 10 sentences, then swap with a partner for one improvement.' Collect explanations as the summative evidence for the sub-strand.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Model comparison and revision (SEP 2) with Claim-Evidence-Reasoning (SEP 6): learners contrast the Lesson 1 model with the final model to see how evidence, calculation and wave ideas have strengthened their understanding. Writing an individual final explanation consolidates learning and applies the same principles to all four sites and to other wave behaviours.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Use a rubric with four levels. Level 4: all four sites correctly modelled with d = vt/2 numbers; explains echo versus reverberation; links strength of echo to reflection percentage, absorption and spreading; includes the law of reflection and at least four other wave behaviours; proposes noise reduction with evidence. Level 3: three of these. Level 2: describes reflection and delay but ignores absorption or the 17 m condition. Level 1: describes only that sound bounces. Look for misconceptions: 'longer delay means stronger echo', 'echo and reverberation are the same', 'sound travels faster in a hall', 'grass stops sound because it is soft only, without energy absorption'. Target: at least 80% at Level 3 or 4. Peer swap suggestions are recorded as evidence of revision."
      }
    ],
    "teacherReflection": "After the lesson, review the Final Explanations and the revised models. Did learners use d = vt/2 correctly for all four sites (0.50 s, 1.00 s, 0.07 s, 1.76 s) and state that 0.07 s is less than 0.1 s so site C is reverberation not a separate echo? Did they explain site D by spreading and absorption rather than distance alone? Which learners still confuse loudness (amplitude, dB) with pitch (frequency)? Did the noise-reduction designs cite the surface table with evidence? Which DQB questions remain unanswered, and how will you use them in future lessons or a school project on a quieter school? Consider what worked in the model comparison with the Lesson 1 sketch, which scaffolds helped quieter learners, and how to link the noise health message to the community, such as matatu noise on Kisumu and Nairobi roads. Note which groups struggled with time management between design and model building and plan how to adjust the two-period schedule.",
    "summaryTablePrompt": {
      "observed": "We observed that noise levels range from 30 dB (whisper) to 95 dB (matatu with loud music), with 85 dB as the class safe limit for up to 8 hours. We also observed that hard materials such as painted concrete (85% reflected) and wooden board (60%) reflect sound strongly, while curtains (15%) and foam or grass (10%) reflect very little. At the four sites, we saw that Hell's Gate (0.50 s) and Ngong Hills (1.00 s) give clear echoes, the Kisumu hall (0.07 s) gives a loud muddy sound, and the Nairobi open grounds give no echo.",
      "learned": "We learned that noise above safe limits can damage hearing and cause stress and poor concentration, and that absorbing materials and barriers reduce noise and reverberation. We learned that an echo needs a reflector at least 17 m away (delay of at least 0.1 s) and a surface that reflects enough energy, that d = vt/2 with v = 340 m/s, and that reverberation is overlapping reflections that merge and prolong the sound. We also learned how reflection, refraction, diffraction, interference, resonance and the Doppler effect all follow the same wave ideas.",
      "explained": "Sound is a mechanical wave that leaves the shouting learner, reflects from a large hard smooth surface following the law of reflection (i = r from the normal) and returns after t = 2d/v. At Hell's Gate and Ngong Hills the surface is far away (more than 17 m) and hard, so a strong separate echo is heard. In the Kisumu hall the wall is only 12 m away (0.07 s) with many hard surfaces, so reflections overlap the voice, causing loud, muddy reverberation of about 1.2 s, which curtains and foam can reduce by absorbing energy. On the open Nairobi grounds the sound spreads out and is absorbed by grass, and the only big wall is 300 m away (1.76 s), so the returning sound is too faint to hear."
    }
  }
